window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2020-07-1", "source": "2020年07月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the saying \" The best preparation for tomorrow is doing your best today. \" You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "The saying \"The best preparation for tomorrow is doing your best today\" reminds us of a simple yet profound\ntruth: our future is not built by anxious planning alone, but by the concrete effort we invest at this very\nmoment. In my view, giving our best today is precisely how we lay a solid foundation for a brighter tomorrow.\nThis idea holds true in almost every field. Take exam preparation as an example. A student who reviews a little\nevery day, solving problems and memorizing words, gradually accumulates the knowledge that pays off on the\ntest day. In contrast, those who indulge in procrastination and cram at the last minute often panic and fail. The\nsame logic applies to career growth: the skills we sharpen through daily practice quietly prepare us for\nopportunities we cannot yet foresee.\nTherefore, instead of worrying about an uncertain future, we should seize the day and focus on what we can\ncontrol right now. If each of us does our best today, tomorrow will take care of itself. The most reliable\npreparation is always the honest effort we make in the present."}, "translation": {"prompt": "《三国演义》(The Romance of the Three Kingdoms)是中国一部著名的历史小说，写于十四世纪。这部文学作品以三国时期的历史为背景，描写了从公元二世纪下半叶到公元三世纪下半叶的魏、蜀、吴三国之间的战争。小说中刻画了近千个人物和无数的历史事件。这些人物和事件虽然大都基于真实的历史，但都不同程度地被浪漫化和戏剧化了。《三国演义》是一部公认的文学杰作。自面世以来，这部小说不断吸引着一代又一代的读者，并且对中国文化产生了广泛而持久的影响 。", "sample": "The Romance of the Three Kingdoms is a famous Chinese historical novel written in the 14th century. Set against the historical background of the Three Kingdoms period, the work describes the wars among the three states of Wei, Shu and Wu from the latter half of the 2nd century to the latter half of the 3rd century. Nearly a thousand characters and countless historical events are portrayed in the novel. Although these characters and events are mostly based on real history, they have been romanticized and dramatized to varying degrees. The Romance of the Three Kingdoms is an acknowledged literary masterpiece. Since its first appearance, the novel has continuously attracted generations of readers and has exerted a wide and lasting influence on Chinese culture."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2020-07-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "The United Nations issued a report last week warning that humans are destroying nature at such a rate that life on Earth is at risk. When the report came out, it naturally  __26__  headlines. But obviously it didn't hijack the news agenda in the manner of a major terrorist attack or  __27__  of war.\nThe report from the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services (IPBES) is clear on what's at  __28__  and what needs to change. IPBES chair Robert Watson says the \"  __29__  evidence\" presents an \"ominous(凶兆的) picture\". \"The health of ecosystems on which we and all other species depend is  __30__  more rapidly than ever,\" Robert Watson said. \"We are  __31__  the very foundations of our economies, livelihoods, food security, health and quality of life worldwide.\" The report says it's not too late if we make \"transformative change\"—fundamental, system-wide reorganization—at every level from local to global, and we need to focus on how to make that happen.\nFirst, don't indulge in despair, because despair leads to inertia and doing nothing means certain  __32__  . Every action to save nature will improve our collective and personal futures and the only way to respond to a threat of this scale is with  __33__  action rooted in headstrong optimism. Second, we need relentless focus, just like when paramedics(救护人员) arrive on a scene and use the concept of \"triage(伤员鉴别分类)\" to ensure the most  __34__  cases get treated first. Saving the natural world needs that kind of thinking. We don't have the  __35__  to do everything at once. We need to make hard choices.", "matchIntro": "Children Understand Far More About Other Minds Than Long Believed", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Until a few decades ago, scholars believed that young children know very little, if anything, about what others are thinking. Swiss psychologist Jean Piaget, who is credited with founding the scientific study of children's thinking, was convinced that preschool children cannot consider what goes on in the minds of others. The interviews and experiments he conducted with kids in the middle of the 20th century suggested that they were trapped in their subjective viewpoints, incapable of imagining what others think, feel or believe."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Much of the subsequent research on early childhood thinking was highly influenced by Piaget's ideas. Scholars sought to refine his theory and empirically confirm his views. But it became increasingly clear that Piaget seemed to have gravely underestimated the intellectual powers of very young kids before they can make themselves understood by speech. Researchers began to devise ever more ingenious ways of figuring out what goes on in the minds of babies, and the resulting picture of their abilities shows subtle variations. Consequently, the old view of children's egocentric(自我中心的) nature and intellectual weaknesses has increasingly fallen out of favor and become replaced by a more generous position that sees a budding sense not only of the physical world but also of other minds, even in the \"youngest young.\""}, {"label": "C", "text": "Historically, children didn't receive much respect for their mental powers. Piaget not only believed that children were \"egocentric\" in the sense that they were unable to differentiate between their own viewpoint and that of others; he was also convinced that their thinking was characterized by systematic errors and confusions. When playing with others, they don't cooperate because they do not realize there are different roles and perspectives. He was convinced that children literally cannot \"get their act together\": instead of playing cooperatively and truly together, they play side by side, with little regard for others. And when speaking with others, a young child supposedly cannot consider the listener's viewpoint but \"talks to himself without listening to others.\""}, {"label": "D", "text": "Piaget and his followers maintained that children go through something like a dark age of intellectual development before slowly and gradually becoming enlightened by reason and rationality as they reach school age. Alongside this enlightenment develops an ever growing understanding of other persons, including their attitudes and views of the world."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Today, a very different picture of children's mental development emerges. Psychologists continually reveal new insights into the depth of young children's knowledge of the world, including their understanding of other minds. Recent studies suggest that even infants are sensitive to others' perspectives and beliefs."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Part of the motivation to revise some of Piaget's conclusions stemmed from an ideological shift about the origin of human knowledge that occurred in the second half of the 20th century. It became increasingly unpopular to assume that a basic understanding of the world can be built entirely from experience. This was in part prompted by theorist Noam Chomsky, who argued that something as complex as the rules of grammar cannot be picked up from exposure to speech, but is supplied by an inborn \"language faculty.\" Others followed suit and defined further \"core areas\" in which knowledge allegedly cannot be pieced together from experience but must be possessed at birth. One such area is our knowledge of others' minds. Some even argue that a basic knowledge of others' minds is not only possessed by human infants, but must be evolutionarily old and hence shared by our nearest living relatives, the great apes."}, {"label": "G", "text": "To prove that infants know more in this realm than had been acknowledged, researchers needed to come up with innovative ways of showing it. A big part of why we now recognize so much more of kids' intellectual capacities is the development of much more sensitive research tools than Piaget had at his disposal."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Instead of engaging babies in dialog or having them execute complex motor tasks, the newer methods capitalize on behaviors that have a firm place in infants' natural behavior repertoire: looking, listening, sucking, making facial expressions, gestures and simple manual actions. The idea of focusing on these \"small behaviors\" is that they give kids the chance to demonstrate their knowledge implicitly and spontaneously without having to respond to questions or instructions. For example, children might look longer at an event that they did not expect to happen, or they might show facial expressions indicating that they have sympathetic concern for others. When researchers measure these less demanding, and often involuntary, behaviors, they can detect a sensitivity to others' mental states at a much younger age than with the more taxing methods that Piaget and his followers deployed."}, {"label": "I", "text": "In the 1980s, these kinds of implicit measures became customary in developmental psychology. But it took a while longer before these tools were employed to measure children's grasp of the mental lives of others."}, {"label": "J", "text": "In a set of experiments, my colleagues at the University of Southern California and I found evidence that babies can even anticipate how others will feel when their expectations are disappointed. We acted out several puppet(木偶) shows in front of two-year-old children. In these puppet shows, a protagonist (Cookie Monster) left his precious belongings (cookies) on stage and later returned to fetch them. What the protagonist did not know was that an antagonist had come and messed with his possessions. The children had witnessed these acts and attentively watched the protagonist return. We recorded children's facial and bodily expressions. Children bit their lips, wrinkled their nose or wiggled(扭动) in their chair when the protagonist came back, as if they anticipated the bewilderment and disappointment he was about to experience. Importantly, children showed no such reactions and remained calm when the protagonist had seen the events himself and thus knew what to expect. Our study reveals that by the tender age of two, kids not only track what others believe or expect; they can even foresee how others will feel when they discover reality."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Studies like this reveal that there is much more going on in small kids' and even infants' minds than was previously believed. With the explicit measures used by Piaget and successors, these deeper layers of kids' understanding cannot be accessed. The new investigative tools demonstrate that kids know more than they can say: when we scratch beneath the surface, we find an emerging understanding of relations and perspectives that Piaget probably did not dream of."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Despite these obvious advances in the study of young children's thinking, it would be a grave mistake to dismiss the careful and systematic analyses compiled by Piaget and others before the new tests dominated the scene because the original methods revealed essential facts about how children think that the new methods cannot uncover."}, {"label": "M", "text": "There's no consensus in today's science community about how much we can infer from a look, a facial expression or a hand gesture. These behaviors clearly indicate a curiosity about what goes on in the mind of others, and probably a set of early intuitions coupled with a willingness to learn more. They pave the way to richer and more explicit forms of understanding of the minds of others. But they can in no way replace the child's growing ability to articulate and refine her understanding of how people behave and why."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "People often discuss the dangers of too much stress, but lately a very different view of stress is gaining popularity: this view of stress, held by members of the positive stress movement, argues that stress might actually be beneficial. The positive stress movement is made up of people such as Zachary Rapp who are looking for an edge in a competitive world, and Rapp's routine is a good example of followers of the movement. He wakes up most mornings at dawn, goes for a run, sips black coffee while ripping through emails, and then steps into a freezing cold shower. This is a routine designed to reduce the stress of running simultaneously three different health and biotechnology companies for 18 hours a day.\nAlthough Rapp's practices may sound extreme, he is part of a growing movement, consisting largely of tech industry workers who claim that such radical tactics will help them live better and longer. Inspired by influential figures in different fields, including entertainers, athletes, entrepreneurs and scientists, positive stress practitioners seek out some combination of extreme temperatures, restrictive diets, punishing exercise routines and general discomfort.\nRapp argues that positive stress keeps him balanced. In addition to running and freezing showers, Rapp uses ice baths, hot yoga, and unconventional eating practices such as eliminating dairy, sugar, alcohol and various other foods high in carbohydrates. He believes that these practices, which put stress on his body, actually make him feel less stress from work. However, Rapp does not credit anyone in particular for his choices: he said he started using these methods in college, where he got into the habit of taking ice baths to recover from sports. He got back into it while trying to get his three companies off the ground.\nRapp works long hours and sleeps only five to seven hours a night but he said he only gets sick once a year. For him, the difference between day-to-day stress, like the kind we feel when moving apartments, and positive stress is that the latter involves pushing the body to extremes and forcing it to build up a tolerance.\nOne thought leader in the positive stress world is Dutch extreme athlete Wim Hof, who earned the name \"ice man\" for his ability to withstand severe cold using deep breathing exercises. Hof's ideas have become popular among tech industry elites and, thanks to Hof, cold showers are now a trend; indeed, some even call it a form of therapy.\nBut it is important to note that not everyone agrees with these practitioners; indeed, some medical professionals argue that positive stress is not for everyone, and that it might even be dangerous for people who are unhealthy or older.", "47": "People often discuss the dangers of too much stress, but lately a very different view of stress is gaining popularity: this view of stress, held by members of the positive stress movement, argues that stress might actually be beneficial. The positive stress movement is made up of people such as Zachary Rapp who are looking for an edge in a competitive world, and Rapp's routine is a good example of followers of the movement. He wakes up most mornings at dawn, goes for a run, sips black coffee while ripping through emails, and then steps into a freezing cold shower. This is a routine designed to reduce the stress of running simultaneously three different health and biotechnology companies for 18 hours a day.\nAlthough Rapp's practices may sound extreme, he is part of a growing movement, consisting largely of tech industry workers who claim that such radical tactics will help them live better and longer. Inspired by influential figures in different fields, including entertainers, athletes, entrepreneurs and scientists, positive stress practitioners seek out some combination of extreme temperatures, restrictive diets, punishing exercise routines and general discomfort.\nRapp argues that positive stress keeps him balanced. In addition to running and freezing showers, Rapp uses ice baths, hot yoga, and unconventional eating practices such as eliminating dairy, sugar, alcohol and various other foods high in carbohydrates. He believes that these practices, which put stress on his body, actually make him feel less stress from work. However, Rapp does not credit anyone in particular for his choices: he said he started using these methods in college, where he got into the habit of taking ice baths to recover from sports. He got back into it while trying to get his three companies off the ground.\nRapp works long hours and sleeps only five to seven hours a night but he said he only gets sick once a year. For him, the difference between day-to-day stress, like the kind we feel when moving apartments, and positive stress is that the latter involves pushing the body to extremes and forcing it to build up a tolerance.\nOne thought leader in the positive stress world is Dutch extreme athlete Wim Hof, who earned the name \"ice man\" for his ability to withstand severe cold using deep breathing exercises. Hof's ideas have become popular among tech industry elites and, thanks to Hof, cold showers are now a trend; indeed, some even call it a form of therapy.\nBut it is important to note that not everyone agrees with these practitioners; indeed, some medical professionals argue that positive stress is not for everyone, and that it might even be dangerous for people who are unhealthy or older.", "48": "People often discuss the dangers of too much stress, but lately a very different view of stress is gaining popularity: this view of stress, held by members of the positive stress movement, argues that stress might actually be beneficial. The positive stress movement is made up of people such as Zachary Rapp who are looking for an edge in a competitive world, and Rapp's routine is a good example of followers of the movement. He wakes up most mornings at dawn, goes for a run, sips black coffee while ripping through emails, and then steps into a freezing cold shower. This is a routine designed to reduce the stress of running simultaneously three different health and biotechnology companies for 18 hours a day.\nAlthough Rapp's practices may sound extreme, he is part of a growing movement, consisting largely of tech industry workers who claim that such radical tactics will help them live better and longer. Inspired by influential figures in different fields, including entertainers, athletes, entrepreneurs and scientists, positive stress practitioners seek out some combination of extreme temperatures, restrictive diets, punishing exercise routines and general discomfort.\nRapp argues that positive stress keeps him balanced. In addition to running and freezing showers, Rapp uses ice baths, hot yoga, and unconventional eating practices such as eliminating dairy, sugar, alcohol and various other foods high in carbohydrates. He believes that these practices, which put stress on his body, actually make him feel less stress from work. However, Rapp does not credit anyone in particular for his choices: he said he started using these methods in college, where he got into the habit of taking ice baths to recover from sports. He got back into it while trying to get his three companies off the ground.\nRapp works long hours and sleeps only five to seven hours a night but he said he only gets sick once a year. For him, the difference between day-to-day stress, like the kind we feel when moving apartments, and positive stress is that the latter involves pushing the body to extremes and forcing it to build up a tolerance.\nOne thought leader in the positive stress world is Dutch extreme athlete Wim Hof, who earned the name \"ice man\" for his ability to withstand severe cold using deep breathing exercises. Hof's ideas have become popular among tech industry elites and, thanks to Hof, cold showers are now a trend; indeed, some even call it a form of therapy.\nBut it is important to note that not everyone agrees with these practitioners; indeed, some medical professionals argue that positive stress is not for everyone, and that it might even be dangerous for people who are unhealthy or older.", "49": "People often discuss the dangers of too much stress, but lately a very different view of stress is gaining popularity: this view of stress, held by members of the positive stress movement, argues that stress might actually be beneficial. The positive stress movement is made up of people such as Zachary Rapp who are looking for an edge in a competitive world, and Rapp's routine is a good example of followers of the movement. He wakes up most mornings at dawn, goes for a run, sips black coffee while ripping through emails, and then steps into a freezing cold shower. This is a routine designed to reduce the stress of running simultaneously three different health and biotechnology companies for 18 hours a day.\nAlthough Rapp's practices may sound extreme, he is part of a growing movement, consisting largely of tech industry workers who claim that such radical tactics will help them live better and longer. Inspired by influential figures in different fields, including entertainers, athletes, entrepreneurs and scientists, positive stress practitioners seek out some combination of extreme temperatures, restrictive diets, punishing exercise routines and general discomfort.\nRapp argues that positive stress keeps him balanced. In addition to running and freezing showers, Rapp uses ice baths, hot yoga, and unconventional eating practices such as eliminating dairy, sugar, alcohol and various other foods high in carbohydrates. He believes that these practices, which put stress on his body, actually make him feel less stress from work. However, Rapp does not credit anyone in particular for his choices: he said he started using these methods in college, where he got into the habit of taking ice baths to recover from sports. He got back into it while trying to get his three companies off the ground.\nRapp works long hours and sleeps only five to seven hours a night but he said he only gets sick once a year. For him, the difference between day-to-day stress, like the kind we feel when moving apartments, and positive stress is that the latter involves pushing the body to extremes and forcing it to build up a tolerance.\nOne thought leader in the positive stress world is Dutch extreme athlete Wim Hof, who earned the name \"ice man\" for his ability to withstand severe cold using deep breathing exercises. Hof's ideas have become popular among tech industry elites and, thanks to Hof, cold showers are now a trend; indeed, some even call it a form of therapy.\nBut it is important to note that not everyone agrees with these practitioners; indeed, some medical professionals argue that positive stress is not for everyone, and that it might even be dangerous for people who are unhealthy or older.", "50": "People often discuss the dangers of too much stress, but lately a very different view of stress is gaining popularity: this view of stress, held by members of the positive stress movement, argues that stress might actually be beneficial. The positive stress movement is made up of people such as Zachary Rapp who are looking for an edge in a competitive world, and Rapp's routine is a good example of followers of the movement. He wakes up most mornings at dawn, goes for a run, sips black coffee while ripping through emails, and then steps into a freezing cold shower. This is a routine designed to reduce the stress of running simultaneously three different health and biotechnology companies for 18 hours a day.\nAlthough Rapp's practices may sound extreme, he is part of a growing movement, consisting largely of tech industry workers who claim that such radical tactics will help them live better and longer. Inspired by influential figures in different fields, including entertainers, athletes, entrepreneurs and scientists, positive stress practitioners seek out some combination of extreme temperatures, restrictive diets, punishing exercise routines and general discomfort.\nRapp argues that positive stress keeps him balanced. In addition to running and freezing showers, Rapp uses ice baths, hot yoga, and unconventional eating practices such as eliminating dairy, sugar, alcohol and various other foods high in carbohydrates. He believes that these practices, which put stress on his body, actually make him feel less stress from work. However, Rapp does not credit anyone in particular for his choices: he said he started using these methods in college, where he got into the habit of taking ice baths to recover from sports. He got back into it while trying to get his three companies off the ground.\nRapp works long hours and sleeps only five to seven hours a night but he said he only gets sick once a year. For him, the difference between day-to-day stress, like the kind we feel when moving apartments, and positive stress is that the latter involves pushing the body to extremes and forcing it to build up a tolerance.\nOne thought leader in the positive stress world is Dutch extreme athlete Wim Hof, who earned the name \"ice man\" for his ability to withstand severe cold using deep breathing exercises. Hof's ideas have become popular among tech industry elites and, thanks to Hof, cold showers are now a trend; indeed, some even call it a form of therapy.\nBut it is important to note that not everyone agrees with these practitioners; indeed, some medical professionals argue that positive stress is not for everyone, and that it might even be dangerous for people who are unhealthy or older.", "51": "Is hunting good or bad for the environment? Like so many hot button issues, the answer to this question depends upon who you ask. On the one hand, some say, nothing could be more natural than hunting, and indeed just about every animal species—including humans—has been either predator or prey at some point in its evolution. And, ironic as it sounds, since humans have wiped out many animal predators, some see hunting as a natural way to reduce the herds of prey animals that now reproduce beyond the environment's carrying capacity.\nOn the other hand, many environmental and animal advocates see hunting as savage, arguing that it is morally wrong to kill animals, regardless of practical considerations. According to Glenn Kirk of the California-based The Animals' Voice, hunting \"causes immense suffering to individual wild animals…\" and is \"irrationally cruel because unlike natural predation(捕食), hunters kill for pleasure …\" He adds that, despite hunters' claims that hunting keeps wildlife populations in balance, hunters' license fees are used to \"manipulate a few game species into overpopulation at the expense of a much larger number of non-game species, resulting in the loss of biological diversity, genetic integrity and ecological balance.\"\nBeyond moral issues, others contend that hunting is not practical. According to the Humane Society of the United States, the vast majority of hunted species—such as waterfowl, rabbits, upland birds and mourning doves—\"provide minimal nutrition and do not require population control.\"\nAuthor Gary E. Varner suggests in his book, In Nature's Interests, that some types of hunting may be morally justifiable while others may not be. Hunting \"designed to secure the aggregate welfare of the target species, the integrity of its ecosystem, or both\"—what Varner terms \"therapeutic hunting\"—is defensible, while subsistence and sport hunting—both of which only benefit human beings—are not.\nRegardless of one's individual stance, fewer Americans hunt today than in recent history. Data gathered by the U. S. Fish & Wildlife Service in 2006 show that only five percent of Americans—some 12.5 million individuals—consider themselves hunters today, down from nine percent in 2001 and 15 percent in 1996.\nPublic support for hunting, however, is on the rise. A 2007 survey by Responsive Management Inc. found that eighty percent of respondents agreed that \"hunting has a legitimate place in modern society,\" and the percentage of Americans indicating disapproval of hunting declined from 22 percent in 1995 to 16 percent in 2007.\nPerhaps matching the trend among the public, green leaders are increasingly advocating cooperation between hunters and environmental groups: After all, both deplore urban sprawl and habitat destruction.", "52": "Is hunting good or bad for the environment? Like so many hot button issues, the answer to this question depends upon who you ask. On the one hand, some say, nothing could be more natural than hunting, and indeed just about every animal species—including humans—has been either predator or prey at some point in its evolution. And, ironic as it sounds, since humans have wiped out many animal predators, some see hunting as a natural way to reduce the herds of prey animals that now reproduce beyond the environment's carrying capacity.\nOn the other hand, many environmental and animal advocates see hunting as savage, arguing that it is morally wrong to kill animals, regardless of practical considerations. According to Glenn Kirk of the California-based The Animals' Voice, hunting \"causes immense suffering to individual wild animals…\" and is \"irrationally cruel because unlike natural predation(捕食), hunters kill for pleasure …\" He adds that, despite hunters' claims that hunting keeps wildlife populations in balance, hunters' license fees are used to \"manipulate a few game species into overpopulation at the expense of a much larger number of non-game species, resulting in the loss of biological diversity, genetic integrity and ecological balance.\"\nBeyond moral issues, others contend that hunting is not practical. According to the Humane Society of the United States, the vast majority of hunted species—such as waterfowl, rabbits, upland birds and mourning doves—\"provide minimal nutrition and do not require population control.\"\nAuthor Gary E. Varner suggests in his book, In Nature's Interests, that some types of hunting may be morally justifiable while others may not be. Hunting \"designed to secure the aggregate welfare of the target species, the integrity of its ecosystem, or both\"—what Varner terms \"therapeutic hunting\"—is defensible, while subsistence and sport hunting—both of which only benefit human beings—are not.\nRegardless of one's individual stance, fewer Americans hunt today than in recent history. Data gathered by the U. S. Fish & Wildlife Service in 2006 show that only five percent of Americans—some 12.5 million individuals—consider themselves hunters today, down from nine percent in 2001 and 15 percent in 1996.\nPublic support for hunting, however, is on the rise. A 2007 survey by Responsive Management Inc. found that eighty percent of respondents agreed that \"hunting has a legitimate place in modern society,\" and the percentage of Americans indicating disapproval of hunting declined from 22 percent in 1995 to 16 percent in 2007.\nPerhaps matching the trend among the public, green leaders are increasingly advocating cooperation between hunters and environmental groups: After all, both deplore urban sprawl and habitat destruction.", "53": "Is hunting good or bad for the environment? Like so many hot button issues, the answer to this question depends upon who you ask. On the one hand, some say, nothing could be more natural than hunting, and indeed just about every animal species—including humans—has been either predator or prey at some point in its evolution. And, ironic as it sounds, since humans have wiped out many animal predators, some see hunting as a natural way to reduce the herds of prey animals that now reproduce beyond the environment's carrying capacity.\nOn the other hand, many environmental and animal advocates see hunting as savage, arguing that it is morally wrong to kill animals, regardless of practical considerations. According to Glenn Kirk of the California-based The Animals' Voice, hunting \"causes immense suffering to individual wild animals…\" and is \"irrationally cruel because unlike natural predation(捕食), hunters kill for pleasure …\" He adds that, despite hunters' claims that hunting keeps wildlife populations in balance, hunters' license fees are used to \"manipulate a few game species into overpopulation at the expense of a much larger number of non-game species, resulting in the loss of biological diversity, genetic integrity and ecological balance.\"\nBeyond moral issues, others contend that hunting is not practical. According to the Humane Society of the United States, the vast majority of hunted species—such as waterfowl, rabbits, upland birds and mourning doves—\"provide minimal nutrition and do not require population control.\"\nAuthor Gary E. Varner suggests in his book, In Nature's Interests, that some types of hunting may be morally justifiable while others may not be. Hunting \"designed to secure the aggregate welfare of the target species, the integrity of its ecosystem, or both\"—what Varner terms \"therapeutic hunting\"—is defensible, while subsistence and sport hunting—both of which only benefit human beings—are not.\nRegardless of one's individual stance, fewer Americans hunt today than in recent history. Data gathered by the U. S. Fish & Wildlife Service in 2006 show that only five percent of Americans—some 12.5 million individuals—consider themselves hunters today, down from nine percent in 2001 and 15 percent in 1996.\nPublic support for hunting, however, is on the rise. A 2007 survey by Responsive Management Inc. found that eighty percent of respondents agreed that \"hunting has a legitimate place in modern society,\" and the percentage of Americans indicating disapproval of hunting declined from 22 percent in 1995 to 16 percent in 2007.\nPerhaps matching the trend among the public, green leaders are increasingly advocating cooperation between hunters and environmental groups: After all, both deplore urban sprawl and habitat destruction.", "54": "Is hunting good or bad for the environment? Like so many hot button issues, the answer to this question depends upon who you ask. On the one hand, some say, nothing could be more natural than hunting, and indeed just about every animal species—including humans—has been either predator or prey at some point in its evolution. And, ironic as it sounds, since humans have wiped out many animal predators, some see hunting as a natural way to reduce the herds of prey animals that now reproduce beyond the environment's carrying capacity.\nOn the other hand, many environmental and animal advocates see hunting as savage, arguing that it is morally wrong to kill animals, regardless of practical considerations. According to Glenn Kirk of the California-based The Animals' Voice, hunting \"causes immense suffering to individual wild animals…\" and is \"irrationally cruel because unlike natural predation(捕食), hunters kill for pleasure …\" He adds that, despite hunters' claims that hunting keeps wildlife populations in balance, hunters' license fees are used to \"manipulate a few game species into overpopulation at the expense of a much larger number of non-game species, resulting in the loss of biological diversity, genetic integrity and ecological balance.\"\nBeyond moral issues, others contend that hunting is not practical. According to the Humane Society of the United States, the vast majority of hunted species—such as waterfowl, rabbits, upland birds and mourning doves—\"provide minimal nutrition and do not require population control.\"\nAuthor Gary E. Varner suggests in his book, In Nature's Interests, that some types of hunting may be morally justifiable while others may not be. Hunting \"designed to secure the aggregate welfare of the target species, the integrity of its ecosystem, or both\"—what Varner terms \"therapeutic hunting\"—is defensible, while subsistence and sport hunting—both of which only benefit human beings—are not.\nRegardless of one's individual stance, fewer Americans hunt today than in recent history. Data gathered by the U. S. Fish & Wildlife Service in 2006 show that only five percent of Americans—some 12.5 million individuals—consider themselves hunters today, down from nine percent in 2001 and 15 percent in 1996.\nPublic support for hunting, however, is on the rise. A 2007 survey by Responsive Management Inc. found that eighty percent of respondents agreed that \"hunting has a legitimate place in modern society,\" and the percentage of Americans indicating disapproval of hunting declined from 22 percent in 1995 to 16 percent in 2007.\nPerhaps matching the trend among the public, green leaders are increasingly advocating cooperation between hunters and environmental groups: After all, both deplore urban sprawl and habitat destruction.", "55": "Is hunting good or bad for the environment? Like so many hot button issues, the answer to this question depends upon who you ask. On the one hand, some say, nothing could be more natural than hunting, and indeed just about every animal species—including humans—has been either predator or prey at some point in its evolution. And, ironic as it sounds, since humans have wiped out many animal predators, some see hunting as a natural way to reduce the herds of prey animals that now reproduce beyond the environment's carrying capacity.\nOn the other hand, many environmental and animal advocates see hunting as savage, arguing that it is morally wrong to kill animals, regardless of practical considerations. According to Glenn Kirk of the California-based The Animals' Voice, hunting \"causes immense suffering to individual wild animals…\" and is \"irrationally cruel because unlike natural predation(捕食), hunters kill for pleasure …\" He adds that, despite hunters' claims that hunting keeps wildlife populations in balance, hunters' license fees are used to \"manipulate a few game species into overpopulation at the expense of a much larger number of non-game species, resulting in the loss of biological diversity, genetic integrity and ecological balance.\"\nBeyond moral issues, others contend that hunting is not practical. According to the Humane Society of the United States, the vast majority of hunted species—such as waterfowl, rabbits, upland birds and mourning doves—\"provide minimal nutrition and do not require population control.\"\nAuthor Gary E. Varner suggests in his book, In Nature's Interests, that some types of hunting may be morally justifiable while others may not be. Hunting \"designed to secure the aggregate welfare of the target species, the integrity of its ecosystem, or both\"—what Varner terms \"therapeutic hunting\"—is defensible, while subsistence and sport hunting—both of which only benefit human beings—are not.\nRegardless of one's individual stance, fewer Americans hunt today than in recent history. Data gathered by the U. S. Fish & Wildlife Service in 2006 show that only five percent of Americans—some 12.5 million individuals—consider themselves hunters today, down from nine percent in 2001 and 15 percent in 1996.\nPublic support for hunting, however, is on the rise. A 2007 survey by Responsive Management Inc. found that eighty percent of respondents agreed that \"hunting has a legitimate place in modern society,\" and the percentage of Americans indicating disapproval of hunting declined from 22 percent in 1995 to 16 percent in 2007.\nPerhaps matching the trend among the public, green leaders are increasingly advocating cooperation between hunters and environmental groups: After all, both deplore urban sprawl and habitat destruction."}, "questions": [{"id": "2020-07-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn about Anna Sanchez?", "options": ["A) She is a great athlete.", "B) She is a famed speaker.", "C) She is a famous scientist.", "D) She is a noted inventor."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） She is a great athlete.\n【定位】Mrs Anna Sanchez is a three-time Olympic champion and author of the new book To\nthe Edge.\n安娜·桑切斯女士是一位三届奥运冠军，也是新书《抵达极限》的作者。\n【信号】开场介绍嘉宾的直接陈述。主持人开场介绍嘉宾时直接给出身份 a three-time Olympic\nchampion and author，这是访谈交代人物的固定位置，无需转折提示，听到头衔即定位。\n【替换】a three-time Olympic champion ↔ a great athlete\n三届奥运冠军=了不起的运动员（具体→概括）\n【排除】B) 过度推断\n她确是 author of the new book，又正在节目里侃侃而谈，易让人误推成 She is a famed\nspeaker.；但『写书的人/受访者』不等于『著名演说家』，原文从未称她为 speaker。\n借用原文：author of the new book To the Edge\nC) 原词陷阱\n访谈通篇是 science、scientific knowledge，极易让人听到『科学』就选 She is a famous\nscientist.；但她是『谈论科学如何帮助运动员』的运动员，不是科学家本人。\n借用原文：what has changed is the scientific knowledge\nD) 原词陷阱\n她反复谈 science and technology，易诱人选 She is a noted inventor.；但谈论科技的人\n不等于发明科技的人，原文未称她为 inventor。\n借用原文：science and technology have helped push humans to the edge", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What is the woman's book mainly about?", "options": ["A) How knowledge of human biochemistry has been evolving.", "B) How nutrition helps athletes' performance in competitions.", "C) How scientific training enables athletes to set new records.", "D) How technology has helped athletes to scale new heights."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） How technology has helped athletes to scale new heights.\n【定位】The book is about how science and technology have helped push humans to the edge of their\nphysical abilities.\n这本书讲的是科学与技术如何帮助人类把自身的身体能力推向极限。\n【信号】被问书名含义时的总括应答。主持人请她解释书名，她答 The book is about how…——\nThe book is about 是直接给主旨的总括句，正是被问者对『书讲什么』的应答落点。\n【替换】science and technology have helped push humans to the edge of their physical abilities ↔\ntechnology has helped athletes to scale new heights\n推向身体极限=登上新高度（同义改写）\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nHow knowledge of human biochemistry has been evolving. 借原文 human\nbiochemistry 一词设套；biochemistry 只是书里提到的一个知识侧面，绝非全书主旨，以\n偏概全。\n借用原文：our knowledge today of human biochemistry, nutrition and mechanics\nB) 原词陷阱\nHow nutrition helps athletes' performance in competitions. 借原文 nutrition 设套；营\n养只是书里并列提到的一项，不能代表全书，范围被缩小。\n借用原文：human biochemistry, nutrition and mechanics\nC) 偷换概念\nHow scientific training enables athletes to set new records. 杂糅了原文的 scientific 与\n后文 sports records being broken；但书主旨是『科技把人推向极限』这一宽命题，破纪\n录只是其表现之一，且原文强调的是 technology 而非 training。\n借用原文：sports records being broken that could only be broken with the aid of technology", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What has changed in the past thousands of years?", "options": ["A) Our physical structures.", "B) Our scientific knowledge.", "C) Our biochemical process.", "D) Our concept of nutrition."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Our scientific knowledge.\n【定位】I believe that while our bodies have not changed in thousands of years, what has changed is\nthe scientific knowledge.\n我认为，几千年来我们的身体并没有改变，真正改变的是科学知识。\n【信号】while 引导的『没变 vs 变了』对比。while our bodies have not changed 与 what has\nchanged is the scientific knowledge 形成正反对比，while 是让步对比信号；题问『变了\n什么』，落点在对比里『变了』的那一侧。\n【替换】what has changed is the scientific knowledge ↔ Our scientific knowledge.\n原句明点 scientific knowledge=选项几乎原词\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n原文说 our bodies have not changed，身体属于『没变』的一侧；Our physical\nstructures. 正是身体结构，恰好答反——题问的是『变了』的，它却是『没变』的。\n借用原文：our bodies have not changed in thousands of years\nC) 原词陷阱\nOur biochemical process. 借原文 human biochemistry 设套；biochemistry 只是『知\n识』所含的内容之一，原文明说变的是 scientific knowledge，不是某个生化过程本身。\n借用原文：our knowledge today of human biochemistry, nutrition and mechanics\nD) 原词陷阱\nOur concept of nutrition. 借原文 nutrition 一词诱选；营养同样只是知识涵盖的内容之\n一，并非原文点名『变了』的那一项。\n借用原文：human biochemistry, nutrition and mechanics", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What is the man's concern about the use of technology in sports competitions?", "options": ["A) It may increase the expenses of sports competitions.", "B) It may lead to athletes' over-reliance on equipment.", "C) It may give an unfair advantage to some athletes.", "D) It may change the nature of sports competitions."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It may give an unfair advantage to some athletes.\n【定位】Is there any concern that technology is giving some athletes an unfair advantage over others?\n会不会有这样的担忧：技术正在让某些运动员相比其他人获得不公平的优势？\n【信号】concern 一词直接标示担忧+由男士问出。男士的问句以 Is there any concern that… 直接\n打出 concern 信号，且本题问『男士的担忧』，落点正在他这句问话，而非女士的回答。\n【替换】technology is giving some athletes an unfair advantage over others ↔\nIt may give an unfair advantage to some athletes\n近原句重述，仅时态/语序微调\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt may increase the expenses of sports competitions. 谈比赛开支，全篇没有任何关于\n花钱/成本的内容，纯属凭空添加。\nB) 过度推断\n女士回答里提到 Skis 这类装备，易诱人推出 It may lead to athletes' over-reliance on\nequipment.；但原文谈滑雪板是讲技术进步，从未说运动员『过度依赖装备』，是凭装备\n话题硬推的隐患。\n借用原文：Skis, for example, went from being made of wood to a metal alloy\nD) 过度推断\nIt may change the nature of sports competitions. 说法宏大，像是对 unfair advantage\n的拔高引申；但男士担忧的是『公平』这一具体点，原文从未说科技会『改变比赛本质』。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the woman think is required to be successful in international trade?", "options": ["A) Experience.", "B) Flexibility.", "C) Family background.", "D) Business connections."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Flexibility.\n【定位】Trends and demand come and go. So one needs to be very flexible to succeed in this industry.\n潮流和需求来来去去。所以要在这一行成功，人就必须非常灵活。\n【信号】needs to be… to succeed 的\"必需\"句式。答案信号是表\"必需\"的句式 one needs to be\nvery flexible to succeed in this industry——needs to be… 直接点出成功所必须具备的\n那一项，正是 Q5\"required to be successful\"对应的落点。\n【替换】one needs to be very flexible ↔ Flexibility.\n形容词 flexible 转名词 Flexibility\n【排除】A) 未提及\nExperience. 是凭常识\"显然要有\"的项，但录音从没说成功\"需要\"经验；她只在讲资历时旁\n及做了一辈子，并未把经验列为\"required to be successful\"。\nC) 原词陷阱\nFamily background. 听着像录音里 it runs in the family(这行在家里传承)，于是\"听到家庭\n就想选\"；但那句只是介绍她的出身经历，回答的是\"她怎么入行\"，并非\"成功所必需\"，与\nQ5 问的不是一回事。\n借用原文：it runs in the family\nD) 原词陷阱\nBusiness connections. 对应录音后文 I've made great business contacts over time，但\n她把人脉说成\"做久了的好处\"(benefit)，是结果不是\"成功的必需条件\"；且这话出现在 Q7\n附近，搬到 Q5 来答属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：I've made great business contacts over time", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the woman say is special about her way of doing trade?", "options": ["A) Buying directly from factories.", "B) Shipping goods in bulk by sea.", "C) Having partners in many parts of the world.", "D) Using the same container back and forth."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Using the same container back and forth.\n【定位】I now import furniture from China into Italy and foods from Italy into China. I even use the\nsame container. It's a very efficient way of conducting trade.\n我现在把家具从中国进口到意大利，把食品从意大利进口到中国。我甚至用同一个集装箱。\n这是一种非常高效的贸易方式。\n【信号】even 的强调语气 + \"非常高效\"评价。答案信号是强调词 I even use the same container\n里的 even(甚至)，加上紧跟的 It's a very efficient way 自评\"非常高效\"——even 把这个做\n法特意拎出来当作不寻常之处，正是 Q6 的\"special\"。\n【替换】I even use the same container ↔ Using the same container back and forth.\n\"用同一集装箱\"概括为\"往返复用\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nBuying directly from factories. 听着像录音 I source mid-century modern furniture\nfrom different factories，于是\"听到工厂就想选\"；但从工厂进货是常规做法、她也没说这\n点特别，与 Q6 问的\"special\"不符。\n借用原文：from different factories in China\nB) 未提及\nShipping goods in bulk by sea. 是外贸最普通的做法，听着合理，但录音通篇没说\"海\n运\"或\"大宗\"，属凭常识脑补、原文未提。\nC) 原词陷阱\nHaving partners in many parts of the world. 借用了录音 I possess a network of\nclients and partners 的\"伙伴\"原词，但录音说的合作伙伴只在 in both countries(两国)，\n选项却扩成 many parts of the world(世界许多地方)，范围被放大且这是 Q7 附近的内\n容，搬来答 Q6 也不对路。\n借用原文：partners in both countries", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the woman have in both Italy and China?", "options": ["A) Warehouses.", "B) Factories.", "C) Investors.", "D) Retailers."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Warehouses.\n【定位】I have a warehouse in Genova, Italy and another in Shanghai.\n我在意大利的热那亚有一个仓库，在上海还有另一个。\n【信号】I have… and another in… 的两地并列。答案信号是\"两地各有一个\"的并列句式 I have a\nwarehouse in Genova, Italy and another in Shanghai——a warehouse… and\nanother… 明确说出两国各有一个仓库，正对 Q7 的\"in both Italy and China\"。\n【替换】a warehouse in Genova, Italy and another in Shanghai ↔ Warehouses.\n单数 a warehouse + another 合为复数\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nFactories. 盗用录音 from different factories in China 的高频原词，\"听到工厂就想选\"；\n但工厂是她进货的对象、属于别人，并非她在两地都\"拥有\"的东西，与 Q7 的 have 不符。\n借用原文：from different factories in China\nC) 未提及 Investors. 全篇没有出现，录音里没有任何关于投资人的内容，纯属未提及。\nD) 与原文相反\nRetailers.(零售商)与录音相反：M 说她 sell both lines of products wholesale(批发销\n售)、她答 Of course，说明走的是批发渠道，不是零售；且她\"拥有\"的是仓库，不是零售\n商。\n借用原文：sell both lines of products wholesale", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the woman say makes furniture marginally more profitable?", "options": ["A) Trendy style.", "B) Unique design.", "C) Lower import duties.", "D) Lower shipping costs."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Lower import duties.\n【定位】Furniture is marginally more profitable, mostly because it enjoys lower customs duties.\n家具的利润略微高一些，主要是因为它享有较低的关税。\n【信号】mostly because 的因果信号。答案信号是因果连词 mostly because it enjoys lower\ncustoms duties 里的 because——它直接引出\"利润略高\"的原因，正是 Q8 要的归因\n落点。\n【替换】lower customs duties ↔ Lower import duties.\ncustoms duties = import duties 同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTrendy style. 听着像录音 affordable, yet stylish wooden furniture 的 stylish，于是\"听到\n时髦就想选\"；但那句讲的是家具在意大利\"销量增长\"(好卖)，不是\"利润略高的原因\"，把\"好\n卖\"当成了\"更赚钱\"。\n借用原文：affordable, yet stylish wooden furniture\nB) 未提及\nUnique design. 与 A 同属\"产品卖点\"类，但录音根本没说家具\"设计独特\"，更没把它当作\n利润高的原因，属未提及。\nD) 偷换概念\nLower shipping costs.(更低运费)与 C 极易混，但录音明说原因是 lower customs\nduties(关税)，从未把利润高归于运费；shipping 一词虽在别处出现，这里却答非所问。\n借用原文：lower customs duties", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the passage say about humour in the workplace?", "options": ["A) It helps employees to reduce their stress.", "B) It prevents employees from feeling bored.", "C) It strengthens harmony among employees.", "D) It helps employees to view things positively."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （主旨题） It helps employees to reduce their stress.\n【定位】Humour unlocks the office prison because it lets adults bring some of their childlike spirit to\nthe job.\n幽默打开了办公室这座监狱，因为它让成年人把一些孩童般的精神带到工作中。\n【信号】开篇点题句 + 后文减压主线。首段把职场比作 a five-day prison from which they are\nparoled every Friday（把工作日比作要熬到周五才获释的监狱），紧接一句 Humour\nunlocks the office prison——开篇用\"幽默解开办公室监狱\"点题；其后各段（P3 谈\noutlet for stress、P4 谈 uncork their bottled-up resentments）持续指向缓解压力，主\n旨主线就是\"减压\"。\n【替换】Humour unlocks the office prison ↔ reduce their stress\n比喻还原为字面\nthere is often no outlet for stress ↔ reduce their stress\n提供出口=减压\n【排除】B) 未提及\nIt prevents employees from feeling bored. 说幽默防止员工无聊，但全篇只讲减压、释\n放怨气、提升效率，从未提\"无聊（bored）\"这回事，是凭空添加的功能。\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt strengthens harmony among employees. 借了文中反复出现的 employees 一词制造\n熟悉感，但录音谈的是减压与效率，没有讲幽默增进员工间\"和谐（harmony）\"，方向跑\n偏。\n借用原文：satisfied and productive employees\nD) 过度推断\nIt helps employees to view things positively. 看似与\"幽默\"沾边，但录音从未说幽默让\n人\"积极看待事物（view things positively）\"，这是从减压主题再往前推一步的引申，原文\n没有落脚点。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the study by Howard Pollio show?", "options": ["A) Weekends are conducive to reducing stress.", "B) Humor is vital to interpersonal relationships.", "C) All workers experience some emotional stress.", "D) Humor can help workers excel at routine tasks."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Humor can help workers excel at routine tasks.\n【定位】Pollio conducted a study that proved humour can help workers excel at routine\nproduction tasks.\n波利奥做了一项研究，证明幽默能帮助员工在日常生产任务上表现出色。\n【信号】专名 Pollio + proved 引出结论。录音先报出处 According to Howard Pollio，随后\nPollio conducted a study that proved 用 proved 直接引出研究结论——人名加\"证明\n了\"是答案句的明确信号，结论就在这句里。\n【替换】humour can help workers excel at routine production tasks ↔\nHumour can help workers excel at routine tasks\n近乎逐词对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nWeekends are conducive to reducing stress. 里的 weekends 容易让人联想到首段\nparoled every Friday 那个比喻，但录音只是用周五打比方，从没说\"周末有助于减压\"这个\n论断，更不是 Pollio 研究的结论。\n借用原文：paroled every Friday\nB) 未提及\nHumor is vital to interpersonal relationships. 谈幽默对人际关系重要，听上去合理，但\nPollio 的研究讲的是\"工作表现\"，全篇也没把幽默与\"人际关系（interpersonal\nrelationships）\"挂钩，属无中生有。\nC) 过度推断\nAll workers experience some emotional stress. 用 All 把范围放到\"所有员工\"，录音虽多\n次谈压力，却没说\"所有员工都有情绪压力\"，更不是 Pollio 研究的结论；绝对词常是陷阱。\n借用原文：there is often no outlet for stress", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What can Kodak's employees do in the Humour Room?", "options": ["A) Smash the toys to release their bottled-up resentments.", "B) Take the boss doll apart as long as they reassemble it.", "C) Design and install stress-reducing gadgets.", "D) Strike at the boss doll as hard as they like."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Take the boss doll apart as long as they reassemble it.\n【定位】Employees can take the doll apart as long as they put its arms and legs back in place.\n只要把玩偶的胳膊和腿装回原位，员工就可以把它拆开。\n【信号】条件状语 as long as 是落点。答案句的灵魂在 as long as they put its arms and legs\nback in place——as long as（只要……）是个条件限定，告诉你\"可以拆，但前提是装回\n去\"；听力里这种条件/限定词后面往往就是出题的精确落点，必须听全。\n【替换】take the doll apart ↔ Take the boss doll apart\n拆开动作对应\nas long as they put its arms and legs back in place ↔ as long as they reassemble it\n装回=重新组装\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nSmash the toys to release their bottled-up resentments. 把 P4 的 toy store 与 P4 的\nuncork their bottled-up resentments 两处原词拼在一起，但录音说的是\"拆开（take\napart）并装回\"，绝非\"砸碎（smash）\"，动作被改狠了。\n借用原文：uncork their bottled-up resentments\nC) 原词陷阱\nDesign and install stress-reducing gadgets. 借了原文 stress-reducing gadgets 一词，\n但录音说这些装置是房间里现成的陈设（the room's many stress-reducing gadgets），\n员工是去用、不是去\"设计安装（design and install）\"，把现成品说成员工自造，动作主体\n与行为都错位。\n借用原文：the room's many stress-reducing gadgets\nD) 与原文相反\nStrike at the boss doll as hard as they like. 围着真实存在的 boss doll，且 as hard as\nthey like（想多狠就多狠）很诱人，但录音明确是 take apart（拆开）且必须 put arms\nand legs back in place（装回），既不是\"使劲打\"，更没有\"随心所欲、无须复原\"——条\n件被抹掉了。\n借用原文：as long as they put its arms and legs back in place", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the speaker say has aroused public interest?", "options": ["A) The recent finding of a changed gene in obese mice.", "B) A breakthrough in understanding gene modification.", "C) A newly discovered way for people to lose weight.", "D) The self-repairing ability of a gene in obese mice."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The recent finding of a changed gene in obese mice.\n【定位】Public interest was aroused by the latest discovery of a changed gene in obese mice.\n公众的兴趣是被肥胖小鼠体内一个发生改变的基因这一最新发现所引起的。\n【信号】题干原词 aroused 对应开篇句。篇章开头第一句 Public interest was aroused by the\nlatest discovery of a changed gene in obese mice. 直接出现题干里的 aroused 与\npublic interest，开篇点题句就是答案句。\n【替换】the latest discovery of a changed gene in obese mice ↔\nThe recent finding of a changed gene in obese mice.\nlatest=recent、discovery=finding\n【排除】B) 过度推断\n录音只说发现了一个改变的基因，从未称其为 A breakthrough in understanding gene\nmodification.；breakthrough 与 gene modification 都是原文没有的拔高说法，把一次\n发现夸成理解上的突破，推过了头。\nC) 偷换概念\nA newly discovered way for people to lose weight. 借用了原文 discovery 的味道，却\n把对象从小鼠的基因偷换成给人减肥的方法；原文 a changed gene in obese mice 讲的是\n小鼠基因，根本没说找到了减肥办法。\n借用原文：the latest discovery of a changed gene in obese mice\nD) 原词陷阱\nThe self-repairing ability of a gene in obese mice. 借了 gene 与 obese mice 这些原\n词，但原文从未提到基因有自我修复能力，self-repairing 是凭空捏造的内容。\n借用原文：a changed gene in obese mice", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What do we learn about the changed gene?", "options": ["A) It renders an organism unable to fight diseases.", "B) It prevents the mice's fatty tissues from growing.", "C) It helps organisms adapt to environmental changes.", "D) It renders mice unable to sense when to stop eating."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It renders mice unable to sense when to stop eating.\n【定位】Those with the changed gene may not sense when they have eaten enough or if they have\nsufficient fatty tissue, and thus can't tell when to stop eating.\n带有这个变化基因的个体，可能感知不到自己何时已吃饱、或脂肪组织是否已足够，因而无\n法判断该何时停止进食。\n【信号】因果 thus 引出后果。句中 and thus can't tell when to stop eating 用 thus 把基因的后\n果点出来——感知不到饱、判断不了何时停吃，正是题干要的那个作用，因果信号词之后即\n答案落点。\n【替换】can't tell when to stop eating ↔ It renders mice unable to sense when to stop eating.\ncan't tell=unable to sense\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt renders an organism unable to fight diseases. 借了原文出现过的 organism 一词，但\n原文只说基因影响对饱腹的感知，从未提到它让生物无法抗病，fight diseases 是没说过的\n内容。\n借用原文：tells the organism how fat or full it is\nB) 与原文相反\nIt prevents the mice's fatty tissues from growing. 盗用了原文的 fatty tissue，却把意思\n反了——原文说的是带该基因者感知不到脂肪是否够 if they have sufficient fatty tissue，\n并非这个基因会阻止脂肪生长。\n借用原文：if they have sufficient fatty tissue\nC) 未提及\nIt helps organisms adapt to environmental changes. 又一次借用 organism，但帮助适\n应环境的说法原文完全没有，是凭空加上的功能。\n借用原文：tells the organism how fat or full it is", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does University of Vermont psychologist Esther Rothblum say?", "options": ["A) Human beings have more obesity genes than most mice do.", "B) Half of a person's total weight variation can be controlled.", "C) People are born with a tendency to have a certain weight.", "D) The function of the obesity genes is yet to be explored."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度观点题） People are born with a tendency to have a certain weight.\n【定位】\"This research indicates that people really are born with a tendency to have a certain weight,\njust as they are to have a particular skin color or height.\"\n“这项研究表明，人确实生来就有形成某种体重的倾向，就像他们生来就有特定的肤色或身\n高一样。”\n【信号】人物引述原话。原文 reacted enthusiastically: 之后是 Rothblum 的直接引语，引号内\npeople really are born with a tendency to have a certain weight 即她的观点，被引述\n人原话就是答案句。\n【替换】people really are born with a tendency to have a certain weight ↔\nPeople are born with a tendency to have a certain weight.\n近乎原句保留\n【排除】A) 未提及\nHuman beings have more obesity genes than most mice do. 借了原文人与鼠都有相近\n基因的话题，但 Rothblum 从没比较人和鼠谁的肥胖基因更多，这一数量对比原文没有。\n借用原文：a gene nearly identical to the mouse obesity gene in humans\nB) 张冠李戴\nHalf of a person's total weight variation can be controlled. 把第三段行为遗传学家说的\nless than half of the total weight variation is programmed in the genes 安到了\nRothblum 头上，既换了说话人，又把不到一半由基因决定篡改成半数可控。\n借用原文：less than half of the total weight variation is programmed in the genes\nD) 张冠李戴\nThe function of the obesity genes is yet to be explored. 对应的是原文 The operation\nof this gene in humans has not yet been demonstrated，那是叙述者的客观补充，并非\nRothblum 的话，被错挂到她名下。\n借用原文：The operation of this gene in humans has not yet been demonstrated", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What accounts for Americans' obesity according to a survey by the Center for Disease Control?", "options": ["A) The worsening of natural environment.", "B) The abundant provision of rich foods.", "C) The accelerated pace of present-day life.", "D) The adverse impact of the food industry."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） The abundant provision of rich foods.\n【定位】Such rapid change underlines the role of environmental factors, like the abundance of rich\nfoods, in Americans' overeating.\n如此迅速的变化凸显了环境因素——比如丰富的高热量食物——在美国人暴饮暴食中所起的\n作用。\n【信号】underlines the role 点明成因。原文 Such rapid change underlines the role of\nenvironmental factors, like the abundance of rich foods 用 underlines the role of 这\n一归因表达点出主因是环境因素，并举 rich foods 为例，正是题干要的原因。\n【替换】the abundance of rich foods ↔ The abundant provision of rich foods.\nabundance=abundant provision\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nThe worsening of natural environment. 蹭了原文 environmental factors 里的\nenvironmental，却把环境因素偷换成自然环境恶化；原文说的环境因素指的是食物丰富等\n生活环境，并非自然环境变差。\n借用原文：the role of environmental factors\nC) 未提及\nThe accelerated pace of present-day life. 听着合理，但这项调查归因到的是丰富食物，\n原文从未把肥胖归因于生活节奏加快，pace of life 是没说过的因素。\nD) 偷换概念\nThe adverse impact of the food industry. 借食物话题制造与 B 的混淆，但原文归因的是\nthe abundance of rich foods（丰富食物本身），并没把责任算到食品工业头上，food\nindustry 是替换上去的对象。\n借用原文：the abundance of rich foods", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What quality do men value most concerning friendship, according to a questionnaire response?", "options": ["A) Similarity in interests.", "B) Mental stimulation.", "C) Openness.", "D) Compassion."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Similarity in interests.\n【定位】Men assigned a lower priority to them in favor of similarity in interests, selected by 77% of\nmen, and responsiveness in a crisis, chosen by 61% male respondents.\n男性把这些（情感类品质）排在了较低的位置，转而青睐兴趣相似——77% 的男性选了\n它，以及危机中的回应——61% 的男性受访者选了它。\n【信号】in favor of + 最高占比 77%。信号有两层：一是 in favor of similarity in interests 这\n个\"转而偏爱\"的措辞，直接点出男性的首选；二是紧跟的最高数字 selected by 77% of\nmen，77% 是男性各项里占比最高的，数字最高即\"最看重\"，双重信号锁定 similarity\nin interests。\n【替换】in favor of similarity in interests, selected by 77% of men ↔ Similarity in interests.\n原词直接对应 + 最高占比佐证\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nMental stimulation. 在录音里确实念到，但 Mental stimulation, ranked third in\npopularity by men 说它在男性中只排第三，是男女唯一重合项，并非\"最看重\"，听到这个\n原词就选正中陷阱。\n借用原文：Mental stimulation, ranked third in popularity by men as well as women\nC) 张冠李戴\nOpenness. 录音里念到，但 only 28% named openness as an important quality 说男性\n里只有 28% 看重它，属于女性首选的情感类品质，安到\"男性最看重\"头上是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Among men, only 28% named openness as an important quality\nD) 张冠李戴\nCompassion. 对应录音里的 caring，caring was picked by just 23% 说男性里仅 23% 选\n它，占比最低，远非男性首选，同样是女性向品质被错配给男性。\n借用原文：caring was picked by just 23%", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What do women refer to when speaking of close friendships?", "options": ["A) The willingness to offer timely help.", "B) The joy found in each other's company.", "C) Personal bonds.", "D) Emotional factors."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Emotional factors.\n【定位】It is evident by their selections that when women speak of close friendships they are referring\nto emotional factors, while men emphasize the pleasure they find in a friend's company.\n从她们的选择可以明显看出，女性谈到亲密友谊时指的是情感因素，而男性强调的是从朋友\n的陪伴中获得的乐趣。\n【信号】refer to 与题干对应词 + while 对比。答案句出现题干同款动词 when women speak of\nclose friendships they are referring to emotional factors，refer to 直接呼应题干 refer\nto；而 while 之后讲的是男性，构成男女对比，提醒只取 while 前面属于女性的那半句。\n【替换】they are referring to emotional factors ↔ Emotional factors.\n原句结论词直接等于选项\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nThe willingness to offer timely help. 改写自录音里男性看重的 responsiveness in a\ncrisis—\"someone I can call on for help.\"，那是男性的特征，安给女性属张冠李戴；女性\n谈友谊指的是情感因素而非\"危机援手\"。\n借用原文：they placed such strong emphasis on responsiveness in a crisis—\"someone I can call on\nfor help.\"\nB) 张冠李戴\nThe joy found in each other's company. 几乎照搬录音 while 之后讲男性的 men\nemphasize the pleasure they find in a friend's company，joy=pleasure、company 原\n词都在，但这是男性的特征，回答\"女性所指\"是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：men emphasize the pleasure they find in a friend's company\nC) 未提及\nPersonal bonds. 听起来像和\"情感\"沾边，但录音讲女性时用的是 emotional factors，并\n未出现 personal bonds 这一表述，属未提及的近义诱饵。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What may threaten a friendship for both men and women?", "options": ["A) Failure to keep a promise.", "B) Lack of frankness.", "C) Feelings of betrayal.", "D) Loss of contact."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Feelings of betrayal.\n【定位】As for the hazards of friendship, more than a few relationships have been shattered because\nof cutthroat competition and feelings of betrayal. This applies to both men and women,\nbut unequally.\n至于友谊的隐患，不少关系因激烈的竞争和背叛感而破裂。这一点对男女都适用，只是程度\n不均。\n【信号】hazards 主题句 + applies to both。信号一是 As for the hazards of friendship 点出本\n段讲\"友谊隐患\"正对题干 threaten；信号二是紧接的 This applies to both men and\nwomen，applies to both 与题干 both 严丝合缝，直接圈定 competition 和 betrayal 是\n双方共有的威胁。\n【替换】feelings of betrayal ↔ Feelings of betrayal.\n原句词与选项完全一致且落在 both 范围内\n【排除】A) 未提及\nFailure to keep a promise. 听上去合理，但录音讲隐患时只点了 competition、\nbetrayal、lack of frankness、fear of appearing unmanly，从未提\"违背承诺\"，属未提\n及。\nB) 原词陷阱\nLack of frankness. 录音确实念到，但 men are plagued in almost equal amounts by\ntwo additional issues: lack of frankness 说这是\"额外\"困扰男性的问题，并非男女双方共\n有，与题干 both 不符，是原词陷阱。\n借用原文：men are plagued in almost equal amounts by two additional issues: lack of frankness\nand a fear of appearing unmanly\nD) 未提及\nLoss of contact. 是最符合常识的\"友谊杀手\"，但录音整段讲隐患从未提及\"失联\"，正是预\n读时警惕的那个常识诱饵。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "Where can many of the best dinosaur specimens be found in North America?", "options": ["A) Along the low-lying Colorado River.", "B) At the Dinosaur National Monument.", "C) Along the border of the U.S. and Canada.", "D) At museums of natural history in large cities."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） At museums of natural history in large cities.\n【定位】Many of the best specimens may be seen today at museums of natural history in the larger\ncities of the United States and Canada.\n如今许多最好的标本可以在美国和加拿大较大城市的自然历史博物馆里看到。\n【信号】题干关键词best specimens原样出现。答案句开头 Many of the best specimens may be\nseen today 与题干 best specimens 几乎逐字对应，听到这串和题干同款的词，就知道讲\n者正在回答\"在哪能看到\"，紧跟其后的地点就是答案。\n【替换】at museums of natural history in the larger cities of the United States and Canada ↔\nAt museums of natural history in large cities\n原文短语略作精简，意思不变\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nAlong the low-lying Colorado River. 里的 river 是讲者后文谈骨头来历时才提到的\n（floated down an eastward flowing river），与\"哪里能看到标本\"无关，是把别处的词\n搬来骗\"听到 river 就选\"。\n借用原文：floated down an eastward flowing river\nB) 原词陷阱\nAt the Dinosaur National Monument. 在原文 at what is now the Dinosaur National\nMonument 处出现，但那是骨头被\"发现\"（discovered）的地方，本题问\"最好的标本能在\n哪看到\"，答非所问。\n借用原文：discovered in Utah and Colorado at what is now the Dinosaur National Monument\nC) 偷换概念\n原文只说大城市分布在 the United States and Canada 两国，并未说\"美加边境\"，Along\nthe border of the U.S. and Canada. 凭 United States and Canada 这串原词杜撰出\nborder，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：the larger cities of the United States and Canada", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What occurs to many people when they see the massive bones in the pit wall?", "options": ["A) Volcanic explosions could bring whole animal species to extinction.", "B) Some natural disaster killed a whole herd of dinosaurs in the area.", "C) The pit should be carefully preserved for the study of dinosaurs.", "D) The whole region must have been struck by a devastating flood."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Some natural disaster killed a whole herd of dinosaurs in the area.\n【定位】Many people get the idea from the mass of bones in the pit wall that some disaster such as a\nvolcanic explosion or a sudden flood killed a whole herd of dinosaurs in this area.\n很多人从坑壁上成堆的骨头得出一个想法：某种灾难——比如火山爆发或突发洪水——在这\n一带杀死了一整群恐龙。\n【信号】Many people get the idea直接对应题干人群。答案句以 Many people get the idea 开\n头，与题干 many people 完全对上，这是\"群体想法\"的标志句；它说出的内容（灾难杀死\n整群恐龙）就是\"人们会想到什么\"的答案。\n【替换】some disaster such as a volcanic explosion or a sudden flood ↔ Some natural disaster\n火山/洪水概括为natural disaster\nkilled a whole herd of dinosaurs in this area ↔ killed a whole herd of dinosaurs in the area\n几乎逐字一致\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nVolcanic explosions could bring whole animal species to extinction. 借了原文真念过\n的 volcanic explosion，但原文说的是\"杀死本地一整群恐龙\"，A 却升级成\"使整个物种灭\n绝\"（whole animal species to extinction），范围被夸大，原文从未这么说。\n借用原文：a volcanic explosion or a sudden flood killed a whole herd of dinosaurs\nC) 答非所问\nThe pit should be carefully preserved for the study of dinosaurs. 讲\"该被妥善保护以供\n研究\"，原文虽提到 the largest and best preserved deposit，但那是描述坑保存状况好，\n并非说\"人们看到骨头会想到要保护\"，答非所问。\n借用原文：the largest and best preserved deposit of dinosaurs known today\nD) 过度推断\nThe whole region must have been struck by a devastating flood. 取了原文里作为举例\n之一的 flood，却用 must have（必然）和 whole region（整个地区）把语气和范围都拔\n高，原文只说洪水是人们设想的灾难\"之一\"，没说\"全区必遭毁灭性洪水\"。\n借用原文：a volcanic explosion or a sudden flood killed a whole herd of dinosaurs in this area", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does the speaker suggest about the large number of dinosaur bones found in the pit?", "options": ["A) They floated down an eastward flowing river.", "B) They lay buried deep in the sand for millions of years.", "C) They were skeletons of dinosaurs inhabiting the locality.", "D) They were remains of dinosaurs killed in a volcanic explosion."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They floated down an eastward flowing river.\n【定位】The pit area is a large dinosaur graveyard, not a place where they died. Most of the remains\nprobably floated down an eastward flowing river until they were left on a shallow sandbar.\n这片坑区是一座大型恐龙\"墓地\"，而不是它们死亡的地方。大多数遗骸很可能是顺着一条向\n东流的河漂下来，最后被留在了一处浅沙洲上。\n【信号】not a place where they died是讲者下结论的转折。not a place where they died 用否\n定一句撇清\"原地死亡\"，紧接的 floated down an eastward flowing river 才是讲者给出的\n真正成因。否定+给出新说法，是讲者亮出结论的强信号。\n【替换】Most of the remains probably floated down an eastward flowing river ↔\nThey floated down an eastward flowing river.\n去掉probably后几乎逐字一致\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nThey lay buried deep in the sand for millions of years. 蹭了原文 half buried in the\nsand 的 buried/sand，但原文说的是尸体漂下后\"半埋在沙里直到腐烂\"，并非\"深埋沙中数\n百万年\"；deep 和 for millions of years 都是原文没有的。\n借用原文：they lie, half buried in the sand, until they decay\nC) 与原文相反\nThey were skeletons of dinosaurs inhabiting the locality. 说骨头是\"当地栖居的恐龙\"即\n原地死亡，但讲者明确说 not a place where they died、是漂来的\"墓地\"，C 取的恰是被\n否定的那一边，与原文相反。\n借用原文：The pit area is a large dinosaur graveyard, not a place where they died.\nD) 原词陷阱\nThey were remains of dinosaurs killed in a volcanic explosion. 把第 2 段大众想法里的\na volcanic explosion 搬来，但讲者全篇就是在反驳\"一场灾难一次性害死\"，火山致死正属\n被否定的旧说，D 既盗原词又站错边。\n借用原文：some disaster such as a volcanic explosion or a sudden flood killed a whole herd\nof dinosaurs", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What have young Americans been accused of?", "options": ["A) Indulging in seeking leisure and material comfort.", "B) Attaching too much importance to independence.", "C) Failing to care for parents in the traditional way.", "D) Leaving their parents on the verge of starvation."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Failing to care for parents in the traditional way.\n【定位】Young people in this country have been accused of not caring for their parents the way they\nwould have in the old country. And this is true.\n这个国家的年轻人被指责没有像在老家时那样照顾自己的父母。而这是事实。\n【信号】指责类信号词 accused of 直接领出答案。录音里 have been accused of not caring for\ntheir parents 直接用了与题干同源的 accused of，后面紧跟的 not caring for their\nparents 就是指控内容，这种'指责词 + 后接内容'的结构是答案句的明确信号。\n【替换】not caring for their parents the way they would have in the old country ↔\nFailing to care for parents in the traditional way\nnot caring=Failing to care；老家方式=传统方式\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确实反复讲 an American ideal of independence and autonomy，B 借这个原词诱\n选；但'过度看重独立'录音是用来说老人受其影响（and this is true 之后转向讲老人），并\n非年轻人'被指责'的那件事，属答非所问。\n借用原文：an American ideal of independence and autonomy\nA) 未提及\nIndulging in seeking leisure and material comfort.（沉溺于追求休闲与物质享受）录音\n通篇未提及年轻人贪图享乐，纯属无中生有。\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音里 on the verge of starvation 确实出现过 starvation，D 借它诱选；但原文是说'老人\n自己（we）独居、濒于挨饿'，并非'年轻人害父母挨饿'，张冠李戴且把主体说反了。\n借用原文：on the verge of starvation", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does the speaker say about old people in the United States?", "options": ["A) They have great difficulty living by themselves.", "B) They have little hope of getting any family care.", "C) They have fond memories of their good old days.", "D) They have a sense of independence and autonomy."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They have a sense of independence and autonomy.\n【定位】But it is also true that old people have been influenced by an American ideal of independence\nand autonomy. So we live alone, perhaps on the verge of starvation, in time without friends,\nbut we are independent.\n但同样属实的是，老人受到了美国式独立与自主理想的影响。于是我们独自生活，也许濒于\n挨饿、最终没有朋友，但我们是独立的。\n【信号】转折 But + 结尾 but we are independent 双重强调落点。答案句以 But it is also true\n转折领出，把话题从年轻人转到老人；句末又用 but we are independent 再次转折强调'独\n立'，转折后的内容才是说话人真正想说的老人特征，是答案信号。\n【替换】we are independent ↔ a sense of independence and autonomy\n形容词 independent 名词化为 independence\ninfluenced by an American ideal of independence and autonomy ↔\nhave a sense of independence and autonomy\n受独立理想影响=拥有独立自主感\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音 we live alone, perhaps on the verge of starvation 让人觉得老人'独自生活很困\n难'，A 借此诱选；但说话人这句的落点是 but we are independent——强调的是'独立'而\n非'生活困难'，A 取了转折前的表象、漏了转折后的真意。\n借用原文：we live alone, perhaps on the verge of starvation\nB) 原词陷阱\n全文确有 family care 相关话题（如年轻人不照顾父母），B 借话题词诱选；但题干问的\n是'说话人关于老人说了什么'，本段答案句强调的是老人'独立'，并未说老人'没希望得到家庭\n照顾'，属答非所问。\n借用原文：old people have been influenced by an American ideal\nC) 未提及\nThey have fond memories of their good old days.（怀念过去的好时光）录音从未提及\n老人怀旧，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What is astonishing to the young mothers interviewed by the speaker?", "options": ["A) People in many parts of the world preferred small-sized families.", "B) There have been extended families in most parts of the world.", "C) Many elderly people were unwilling to take care of their grandchildren.", "D) So many young Americans refused to live together with their parents."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） There have been extended families in most parts of the world.\n【定位】They are astonished to hear that in most of the world throughout most of its history, families\nhave been three- or four-generation families, living under the same roof.\n她们惊讶地听到，在世界大多数地区、纵贯其大部分历史，家庭一直是三代或四代同堂、住\n在同一屋檐下的。\n【信号】惊讶标志词 astonished 直接领出答案。录音 They are astonished to hear that 用了与题\n干同源的 astonished，后面 that 从句的内容就是令她们吃惊的事，这种'惊讶词 + that 从\n句'是答案句最明确的信号。\n【替换】families have been three- or four-generation families, living under the same roof ↔\nThere have been extended families\n三四代同堂=extended families（大家庭）\nin most of the world throughout most of its history ↔ in most parts of the world\n世界大多数地区，措辞近乎同义\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音强调历史上多为大家庭，A 却说 preferred small-sized families（偏好小家庭）；说话\n人另有 over-emphasized the small family unit 提到 small family，A 借此原词诱选，但\n把'令人吃惊的事'说反了——惊讶点恰恰是'过去是大家庭'。\n借用原文：over-emphasized the small family unit\nC) 原词陷阱\n全文确有祖辈与孙辈话题（如 cut off the children from their grandparents），C 借\ngrandchildren 诱选；但'许多老人不愿照顾孙辈'录音从未提及，且与令人吃惊的'大家庭'事\n实无关，属答非所问加无中生有。\n借用原文：cut off the children from their grandparents\nD) 答非所问\n录音确有年轻人独立、与父母关系的内容，D 借 young Americans、live together 诱选；\n但题干问'令年轻母亲吃惊的事'，答案句讲的是'历史上多为大家庭'，并非'年轻美国人拒绝与\n父母同住'，属答非所问。\n借用原文：Young people in this country have been accused", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does the speaker say older people try their best to do?", "options": ["A) Leave their younger generations alone.", "B) Avoid being a burden to their children.", "C) Stay healthy by engaging in joyful activities.", "D) View things from their children's perspective."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Avoid being a burden to their children.\n【定位】So in the end, older people have to devote all their energies to \"not being a burden\".\n所以到头来，老人不得不把全部精力都投入到\"不成为负担\"上。\n【信号】竭力类信号 devote all their energies to 直接领出答案。录音 devote all their energies\nto \"not being a burden\" 用 devote all their energies to（倾尽全力于）这一'竭力'信号，\n与题干 try their best 同义，其后引号内的 not being a burden 即老人尽力去做的事，是\n答案落点。\n【替换】devote all their energies to \"not being a burden\" ↔ Avoid being a burden to their children\n竭力做到'不成为负担'=尽力避免成为负担\nthe only thing they can do for their children ↔ to their children\n对子女而言，对象一致\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音 stay out of their way 说的是父母'别挡子女的路'，A 的 Leave their younger\ngenerations alone.（别打扰晚辈）借此诱选；但题干问的是老人'尽力去做'的那件被概括\n的事，答案句明确落在'不成为负担'，A 只是过程中的某句铺垫，非最终落点。\n借用原文：stay out of their way\nC) 偷换概念\n录音 look cheerful when they come to visit 说老人努力'显得开心'，C 借 cheerful/joyful\n的近似诱选；但 C 说成 Stay healthy by engaging in joyful activities.（靠快乐活动保持\n健康），把'显得开心'偷换成'保持健康'，录音并未讲养生，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：look cheerful when they come to visit\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音 perspective 确实出现，D 借它诱选；但原文说的是年轻人被剥夺了 perspective（视\n角），并非'老人努力从子女视角看问题'，主体与含义都对不上，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Young people are being deprived of the thing they need most—perspective", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) capacity", "B) declaration", "C) deteriorating", "D) determined", "E) disaster", "F) eroding", "G) grabbed", "H) inventory", "I) Junction", "J) Monotonous", "K) overwhelming", "L) stagnation", "M) stake", "N) stifled", "O) urgent"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) grabbed\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 it 后缺谓语动词，且 didn't 暗示前句应为过去式\n【依据】grab headlines = 抓住头条，固定搭配\n宾语是 headlines，英语习语 grab headlines / grab the headlines 表示「成为头条新\n闻、抢眼球」，正好对应「报告一出即引发关注」；与下句转折 But obviously it didn't\nhijack the news agenda 中的 hijack 形成「抓住头条 vs 未能劫持议程」的同质对比。\n【竞争词】N) stifled stifled headlines 语义反向，且与下文转折逻辑矛盾\nstifle 是「扼杀、压制」，stifled headlines 意为「压住了头条」，与 naturally（理所当然地）\n的褒义色彩相反，也与「报告确实成了头条但没像恐袭那样劫持议程」的转折逻辑冲突。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 N) —— stifled 与 grabbed 同为 -ed 过去式动词，词性相同。it\nnaturally __(26)__ headlines 处考生若只看「过去式动词 + headlines」就可能误选\nN(stifled)；但 stifle headlines 语义反向，且与下句转折 But obviously it didn't hijack\nthe news agenda 的让步—转折逻辑链不通。防坑口诀：动词题别只看词性，盯住宾语找\n固定搭配（grab headlines）。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) capacity", "B) declaration", "C) deteriorating", "D) determined", "E) disaster", "F) eroding", "G) grabbed", "H) inventory", "I) Junction", "J) Monotonous", "K) overwhelming", "L) stagnation", "M) stake", "N) stifled", "O) urgent"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) declaration\n名词\n【词性槽】并列连词 or 后接同类名词，且后有介词 of war\n【依据】declaration of war = 宣战，与 a major terrorist attack 同属「重大突发新闻」\n前项是 a major terrorist attack（重大恐袭），或与之并列的同类大新闻典型即「宣战」；\ndeclaration of war 是英语固定表达，与 hijack the news agenda（霸占新闻议程）的语\n境贴合：这两类事件才是真能霸占议程的级别。\n【竞争词】H) inventory inventory of war 不成搭配，且与「霸占新闻议程」语境不符\ninventory 多指商业清单，与 war 无固定搭配，也不属于能像恐袭一样霸占议程的事件类型。\nL) stagnation stagnation of war 语义古怪，不成习语\n「战争的停滞」既非英语习语，也与本句强调「能霸占议程的大事」格格不入。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) capacity", "B) declaration", "C) deteriorating", "D) determined", "E) disaster", "F) eroding", "G) grabbed", "H) inventory", "I) Junction", "J) Monotonous", "K) overwhelming", "L) stagnation", "M) stake", "N) stifled", "O) urgent"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) stake\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 at 后接名词，固定搭配 at stake\n【依据】at stake = 处于危险之中，正面呼应「危机」语境\n上一段刚说 life on Earth is at risk，本句承接说报告对「what is __ 和 what needs to\nchange」讲得清楚，「危在旦夕的事物」正是报告关注的对象，at stake 既是固定搭配也\n契合上下文。\n【竞争词】A) capacity at capacity 义为「满载」，与生态危机语境不符\ncapacity 强调容量已用尽，无法表达「重大事物岌岌可危」之意；且 capacity 后文 35 题有更\n贴的去处。\nB) declaration at declaration 不成搭配\ndeclaration 表「声明」，英语里没有 at declaration 的固定用法，语义上也与「报告讲清什么\n事正面临危险」无关。\nE) disaster at disaster 不成搭配，且 disaster 留给 32 题更合适\nat disaster 在英语中并非习惯搭配；disaster 表「灾难」是一个一次性后果名词，与 32 题\ndoing nothing means certain __(32)__ 的「必将酿成大祸」语义完美契合，应该留给那里。\nH) inventory at inventory 不成搭配\ninventory 表「清单/库存」，多用于商业语境，既无 at inventory 习语，也与生态危机话题\n不沾。\nI) Junction 首字母大写，提示用于句首；本空在句中\nJunction 在 ## Choices 中以大写 J 开头，按选词填空惯例提示「该词只可能填在句首」，而本\n空位于句中，词形不匹配；at junction 也非固定搭配。\nL) stagnation at stagnation 不成搭配，语义偏经济停滞\nstagnation 主要表「停滞/不景气」，常出现在经济语境，且没有 at stagnation 这种习语。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) capacity", "B) declaration", "C) deteriorating", "D) determined", "E) disaster", "F) eroding", "G) grabbed", "H) inventory", "I) Junction", "J) Monotonous", "K) overwhelming", "L) stagnation", "M) stake", "N) stifled", "O) urgent"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) overwhelming\n形容词\n【词性槽】冠词 the 与名词 evidence 之间，必为形容词\n【依据】overwhelming evidence = 压倒性证据，强化「ominous picture」的分量\n下半句 presents an \"ominous(凶兆的) picture\"，证据要能呈现凶兆的图景必然分量极\n重，「压倒性证据」与「凶兆图景」在程度上互相支撑，是英语新闻语篇高频搭配。\n【竞争词】C) deteriorating deteriorating evidence 语义不通，且 C 留给 30 题\ndeteriorate 修饰证据没有「证据在恶化」这种说法；且该词在 30 题 is __(30)__ more rapidly\nthan ever 处构成 is deteriorating（生态系统正在恶化），是唯一去处，应该留给那里。\nD) determined determined 多形容人的「坚定」，不修饰 evidence\ndetermined 常说 a determined person / determined effort，几乎不与 evidence 搭配；且\nD 在 33 题 respond to a threat of this scale is with __(33)__ action 修饰 action 更贴。\nF) eroding eroding evidence 不成搭配，且 F 留给 31 题\nerode 表「侵蚀」，不修饰 evidence；F 用在 31 题 We are __(31)__ the very foundations 与\nfoundations 搭配（侵蚀根基）才贴。\nJ) Monotonous 首字母大写仅用于句首，本空在句中\nMonotonous 以大写 M 开头，按选词填空惯例只能填句首；且「单调的证据」语义本身也与\n「凶兆图景」无关。\nO) urgent urgent evidence 不是固定搭配，且 O 留给 34 题\nurgent 修饰需要立刻处理的对象（cases/matters/need），不修饰证据本身；该词在 34 题\nthe most __(34)__ cases get treated first 修饰 cases 才是最佳归宿。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) capacity", "B) declaration", "C) deteriorating", "D) determined", "E) disaster", "F) eroding", "G) grabbed", "H) inventory", "I) Junction", "J) Monotonous", "K) overwhelming", "L) stagnation", "M) stake", "N) stifled", "O) urgent"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) deteriorating\n动词\n【词性槽】be 动词 is 后接现在分词构成进行时\n【依据】deteriorate = 自身状况恶化，主语 health 为不及物对象\n主语是 The health of ecosystems，「健康状况」自身在变坏用不及物的 deteriorate 最\n贴；与 more rapidly than ever 共同表达「比以往任何时候都更快地恶化」。\n【竞争词】F) eroding erode 多为及物，且本句之后正好需要一个及物侵蚀 foundations\nerode 常用搭配是 erode + 宾语（基础、信心等被侵蚀），本句没有宾语，用 erode 不够自\n然；而紧接的 31 题 We are __(31)__ the very foundations of our economies 正好需要\neroding 后接 foundations，F 应留给那里。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) capacity", "B) declaration", "C) deteriorating", "D) determined", "E) disaster", "F) eroding", "G) grabbed", "H) inventory", "I) Junction", "J) Monotonous", "K) overwhelming", "L) stagnation", "M) stake", "N) stifled", "O) urgent"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) eroding\n动词\n【词性槽】are 后接现在分词、后接宾语 the very foundations\n【依据】erode the foundations = 侵蚀根基，固定搭配\nerode 与 foundations 是英语社论高频搭配，表「侵蚀/瓦解（经济、生计等的）根基」；\n本句宾语 the very foundations of our economies, livelihoods, food security, health\nand quality of life worldwide 列出多项「根基」，与 erode 完美对接。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) capacity", "B) declaration", "C) deteriorating", "D) determined", "E) disaster", "F) eroding", "G) grabbed", "H) inventory", "I) Junction", "J) Monotonous", "K) overwhelming", "L) stagnation", "M) stake", "N) stifled", "O) urgent"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) disaster\n名词\n【词性槽】means 的宾语，必为名词\n【依据】certain disaster = 必酿大祸，与「despair → inertia → 做不动 → 灾难」因果链一致\n原句逻辑是「不要绝望，因为绝望导致瘫痪、什么都不做就意味着必然的__」——上文已铺\n垫 life on Earth is at risk 与 \"ominous(凶兆的) picture\"，必然的「灾难」是顺理成章的语\n义落点。\n【竞争词】A) capacity certain capacity 语义不通，且 A 留给 35 题\n「必然的能力」不知所云；且 A 在 35 题 We don't have the __(35)__ to do everything at\nonce. 表「我们没有同时做所有事的能力」才是最佳搭配。\nB) declaration certain declaration 不构成习惯用法，语义不沾\ndeclaration（声明）与「什么都不做的后果」无关，且没有「必然的声明」这种说法。\nH) inventory certain inventory 语义不通\ninventory（清单/库存）是商业词汇，与生态危机后果无关，搭配也不成立。\nI) Junction 首字母大写只填句首，且语义不沾\nJunction 大写形式表明只能位于句首；且「必然的交叉口」语义不通。\nL) stagnation 义近但偏经济停滞，与「灾难」程度感差一档\nstagnation（停滞）在程度与色彩上不如 disaster 直白强烈；本段强调「不行动就万劫不复」，\n需要程度更重的 disaster；且上文已用 inertia（瘫痪）这一近义概念。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 L) —— stagnation 与 disaster 都能扣「不行动的后果」，程度感\n最易踩坑。despair leads to inertia and doing nothing means certain __(32)__ 里，\ninertia（瘫痪）刚铺垫了「停滞」，考生很容易顺势填 L(stagnation) 把它当同义复述；但\n命题人要的是因果链「升级」——certain disaster（必酿大祸）才与全文 life on Earth is\nat risk 的危机感对齐。防坑口诀：因果链尾选程度最重者。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) capacity", "B) declaration", "C) deteriorating", "D) determined", "E) disaster", "F) eroding", "G) grabbed", "H) inventory", "I) Junction", "J) Monotonous", "K) overwhelming", "L) stagnation", "M) stake", "N) stifled", "O) urgent"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) determined\n形容词\n【词性槽】介词 with + ___ + 名词 action，作 action 的定语\n【依据】determined action = 坚定的行动，与 headstrong optimism 同向\n本句强调「以坚强的乐观为根基的__行动」，determined（坚定的、果决的）与\nheadstrong optimism（强硬、坚毅的乐观）在情感色彩上一致；上文 don't indulge in\ndespair 也鼓励行动派态度。\n【竞争词】J) Monotonous 句首大写形式 + 语义反向，双重不符\nMonotonous 大写仅用于句首；且「单调的行动」与「以坚毅乐观为根基」的褒义色彩完全\n相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) capacity", "B) declaration", "C) deteriorating", "D) determined", "E) disaster", "F) eroding", "G) grabbed", "H) inventory", "I) Junction", "J) Monotonous", "K) overwhelming", "L) stagnation", "M) stake", "N) stifled", "O) urgent"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) urgent\n形容词\n【词性槽】the most + ___ + cases，形容词最高级位\n【依据】urgent cases = 急重病例，与 triage 概念精准对应\ntriage 即「伤员鉴别分类」——根据紧急程度决定治疗顺序，「最紧急的病例最先处理」是\ntriage 的字典释义；与本段「优先级行动」主旨完美贴合。\n【竞争词】D) determined determined cases 不通，且 D 留给 33 题\ndetermined 形容「下了决心的（人）」，不形容病例；该词在 33 题修饰 action（坚定的行\n动）才是正宗搭配。\nJ) Monotonous 首字母大写仅可填句首，本空在句中；语义也反\nMonotonous 以大写 M 开头，提示其只能填在句首；且「单调的病例」与急救分诊语境完全\n无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) capacity", "B) declaration", "C) deteriorating", "D) determined", "E) disaster", "F) eroding", "G) grabbed", "H) inventory", "I) Junction", "J) Monotonous", "K) overwhelming", "L) stagnation", "M) stake", "N) stifled", "O) urgent"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) capacity\n名词\n【词性槽】the + ___ + to do 不定式，名词位\n【依据】have the capacity to do = 有做……的能力/资源，框架直击\nhave the capacity to do something 是英语高频框架，表「具备做某事的能力或资源」；\n与下句 We need to make hard choices. 的「资源有限须取舍」逻辑链完美闭合。\n【竞争词】B) declaration have the declaration to do 不成搭配\ndeclaration 不与「to do」这种能力不定式搭配；且语义上「有声明去做……」也讲不通。\nH) inventory have the inventory to do 极少出现且语义不沾\ninventory 多指商品库存清单，have the inventory to do 在英语中并非习惯用法，与「资源不\n足以同时做所有事」的抽象语境不匹配。\nL) stagnation have the stagnation to do 不成搭配，语义反向\nstagnation 是「停滞」，与「做事的能力」语义相反；have the stagnation to do 也不成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Piaget believed that small children could not collaborate with others while playing.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】皮亚杰+玩耍合作\n【定位】He was convinced that children literally cannot \"get their act together\": instead of playing\ncooperatively and truly together, they play side by side, with little regard for others.\n他坚信孩子们简直没法'步调一致'：他们不是合作地、真正一起玩，而是各玩各的，几乎不\n顾及别人。\n【改写】Piaget believed ↔ He was convinced that\n信念动词同义替换\nsmall children ↔ children\n上下位指代等值\ncould not collaborate with others while playing ↔\ninstead of playing cooperatively and truly together, they play side by side, with little regard for\nothers\n否定合作正反话切换\n【辨邻】 A A 段虽然出现 Jean Piaget 这个共享锚点，但它讲的是孩子 incapable of imagining\nwhat others think, feel or believe 这种主观视角问题，没有提到玩耍时合不合作。\nthey were trapped in their subjective viewpoints, incapable of imagining what others think, feel\nor believe\nD D 段也属于皮亚杰一派的论述，但内容集中在 a dark age of intellectual\ndevelopment 这一发展阶段比喻，没有触及玩耍合作。\nchildren go through something like a dark age of intellectual development before slowly and\ngradually becoming enlightened by reason and rationality as they reach school age", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "The author and his colleagues' study shows two-year-olds may be able to predict other people's feelings.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】作者与同事的研究两岁孩子\n【定位】Our study reveals that by the tender age of two, kids not only track what others believe or\nexpect; they can even foresee how others will feel when they discover reality.\n我们的研究表明，两岁这个稚嫩年龄的孩子不仅能追踪他人相信或期待什么，甚至能预见到\n他人发现现实时会有怎样的感受。\n【改写】The author and his colleagues' study ↔\nIn a set of experiments, my colleagues at the University of Southern California and I found\nevidence\n署名信息原样对应\ntwo-year-olds ↔ two-year-old children\n年龄锚点原样复现\nmay be able to predict other people's feelings ↔\nthey can even foresee how others will feel when they discover reality\npredict与foresee同义\n【辨邻】 E E 段提到 Recent studies 与 even infants are sensitive to others' perspectives and\nbeliefs，话题与本题相邻，但既没有第一人称、也没说预测他人感受，被排掉。\nRecent studies suggest that even infants are sensitive to others' perspectives and beliefs", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "In the latter half of the last century, fewer and fewer people believed the basis for our understanding of the world is wholly empirical.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】上世纪下半叶反纯经验论\n【定位】It became increasingly unpopular to assume that a basic understanding of the world can be\nbuilt entirely from experience.\n认为对世界的基本理解可以完全建立在经验之上的这一假设，变得越来越不被接受。\n【改写】In the latter half of the last century ↔ in the second half of the 20th century\n时间状语同义改写\nfewer and fewer people believed ↔ It became increasingly unpopular to assume\n比较级与unpopular同义\nthe basis for our understanding of the world is wholly empirical ↔\na basic understanding of the world can be built entirely from experience\nempirical与from experience等值", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Research conducted by Jean Piaget in the last century suggested babies were insensitive to others' thinking.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】皮亚杰研究表明婴儿不敏感\n【定位】The interviews and experiments he conducted with kids in the middle of the 20th century\nsuggested that they were trapped in their subjective viewpoints, incapable of imagining what\nothers think, feel or believe.\n他在 20 世纪中叶对孩子做的访谈和实验表明，孩子们被困在自己的主观视角里，无法想象\n他人在想什么、感受什么、相信什么。\n【改写】Research conducted by Jean Piaget in the last century ↔\nThe interviews and experiments he conducted with kids in the middle of the 20th century\n上位词Research涵盖访谈实验\nbabies were insensitive to others' thinking ↔\nthey were trapped in their subjective viewpoints, incapable of imagining what others think,\nfeel or believe\n形容词凝练与动词短语展开同义\n【辨邻】 C C 段虽然反复出现 Piaget 与孩子无法考虑他人视角的话题，但用的是 Piaget not\nonly believed that children were \"egocentric\"，讲的是他的信念与论断，而不是陈\n述句所要求的'他自己做的研究表明'这一信息来源。\nPiaget not only believed that children were \"egocentric\" in the sense that they were unable to\ndifferentiate between their own viewpoint and that of others\nD D 段虽含 Piaget and his followers maintained，但讲的是儿童会经历智力黑暗期再\n被理性启蒙，没有提到'他做的研究表明婴儿对他人想法不敏感'。\nPiaget and his followers maintained that children go through something like a dark age of\nintellectual development", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Our improved understanding of babies' intellectual power is attributable to better research tools.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】智力理解归功于更好工具\n【定位】A big part of why we now recognize so much more of kids' intellectual capacities is the\ndevelopment of much more sensitive research tools than Piaget had at his disposal.\n我们今天之所以能识别儿童远比过去多得多的智力，很大程度上是因为研究工具比皮亚杰当\n年所能用到的更敏感得多。\n【改写】Our improved understanding of babies' intellectual power ↔\nwe now recognize so much more of kids' intellectual capacities\n理解提升的同义改写\nattributable to better research tools ↔\nthe development of much more sensitive research tools than Piaget had at his disposal\nbetter与more sensitive同义\n【辨邻】 H H 段虽然也属于'新方法'话题，但写的是具体操作 looking, listening, sucking,\nmaking facial expressions, gestures and simple manual actions，没有明确把'对\n婴儿智力的更好理解'归因到'更好的工具'这一宏观因果关系。\nthe newer methods capitalize on behaviors that have a firm place in infants' natural behavior\nrepertoire: looking, listening, sucking, making facial expressions, gestures and simple\nmanual actions", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "It has been found in recent research that even small babies are sensitive to other people's points of view.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】近期研究小婴儿对他人视角敏感\n【定位】Recent studies suggest that even infants are sensitive to others' perspectives and beliefs.\n近期研究表明，连婴儿也对他人的视角和信念敏感。\n【改写】recent research ↔ Recent studies\nresearch与studies同义\neven small babies ↔ even infants\nsmall babies与infants同义\nare sensitive to other people's points of view ↔ are sensitive to others' perspectives and beliefs\npoints of view与perspectives同义\n【辨邻】 J J 段讲的是 two-year-old children 在木偶实验里的具体表现，没有提出'连 infants 也\n敏感'这种宽泛断言；且没有 Recent studies 这一总括表达。\nChildren bit their lips, wrinkled their nose or wiggled(扭动) in their chair when the protagonist\ncame back\nB B 段讲的是 a more generous position that sees a budding sense not only of the\nphysical world but also of other minds 这种宏观观点取代，没有以 Recent studies\n来报告婴儿对他人视角的敏感这一具体发现。\na more generous position that sees a budding sense not only of the physical world but also of other\nminds, even in the \"youngest young.\"", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Scientists are still debating what inference can be drawn from certain physical expressions of a child.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】科学家仍在争论身体表情推断\n【定位】There's no consensus in today's science community about how much we can infer from a\nlook, a facial expression or a hand gesture.\n对于从一个眼神、一个面部表情或一个手势中可以推断出多少东西，当今科学界尚无共识。\n【改写】Scientists are still debating ↔ There's no consensus in today's science community\n争论与无共识同义\nwhat inference can be drawn from certain physical expressions of a child ↔\nhow much we can infer from a look, a facial expression or a hand gesture\nphysical expressions概括看表情手势", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "The newer research methods focus on infants' simple behaviors instead of requiring them to answer questions.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】新方法关注简单行为不答问题\n【定位】Instead of engaging babies in dialog or having them execute complex motor tasks, the newer\nmethods capitalize on behaviors that have a firm place in infants' natural behavior repertoire:\nlooking, listening, sucking, making facial expressions, gestures and simple manual actions.\n新方法不是让婴儿参与对话或执行复杂的运动任务，而是利用婴儿天然行为库中固有的那些\n行为：看、听、吸吮、做面部表情、手势和简单的手部动作。\n【改写】The newer research methods ↔ the newer methods\n新方法名同义复现\nfocus on infants' simple behaviors ↔\ncapitalize on behaviors that have a firm place in infants' natural behavior repertoire\nfocus on与capitalize on同义\ninstead of requiring them to answer questions ↔\nwithout having to respond to questions or instructions\n排斥答问要求的同义改写\n【辨邻】 G G 段说研究者必须 come up with innovative ways of showing it，强调需要新工\n具，但没有具体讲'新方法用什么简单行为代替答问题'这一操作层信息。\nresearchers needed to come up with innovative ways of showing it", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "With the progress in psychology, the traditional view of children's self-centered nature and limited thinking abilities has become less and less influential.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】自我中心旧观点影响力下降\n【定位】Consequently, the old view of children's egocentric(自我中心的) nature and intellectual\nweaknesses has increasingly fallen out of favor and become replaced by a more generous\nposition that sees a budding sense not only of the physical world but also of other minds, even\nin the \"youngest young.\"\n结果，那种认为儿童本性自我中心、智力薄弱的旧观点越来越失宠，被一种更宽厚的立场所\n取代：这种立场认为，即使在'最小的小娃'身上，也已经萌发了对物质世界、乃至他人心智\n的感知。\n【改写】the traditional view of children's self-centered nature and limited thinking abilities ↔\nthe old view of children's egocentric(自我中心的) nature and intellectual weaknesses\n传统观点与旧观点同义\nhas become less and less influential ↔ has increasingly fallen out of favor\n影响力下降与失宠同义\nWith the progress in psychology ↔\nResearchers began to devise ever more ingenious ways of figuring out what goes on in the\nminds of babies, and the resulting picture of their abilities shows subtle variations\n心理学进步的同义改写\n【辨邻】 E E 段也属心理学进步话题，但讲的是 Recent studies suggest that even infants are\nsensitive to others' perspectives and beliefs 这种新发现，没有直接说'自我中心旧\n观点越来越没影响力'。\nPsychologists continually reveal new insights into the depth of young children's knowledge of\nthe world", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Even though marked advances have been made, it is wrong to dismiss Piaget's fundamental contributions to the study of kids' cognitive abilities.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】不应否定皮亚杰的根本贡献\n【定位】Despite these obvious advances in the study of young children's thinking, it would be a grave\nmistake to dismiss the careful and systematic analyses compiled by Piaget and others before\nthe new tests dominated the scene because the original methods revealed essential facts about\nhow children think that the new methods cannot uncover.\n尽管在儿童思维研究上已有这些显著进展，但若就此否定皮亚杰等人在新方法主导之前所做\n的细致而系统的分析，那将是严重的错误，因为那些原始方法揭示了新方法所无法揭示的关\n于儿童思维的本质事实。\n【改写】Even though marked advances have been made ↔\nDespite these obvious advances in the study of young children's thinking\nEven though与Despite同义让步\nit is wrong to dismiss Piaget's fundamental contributions to the study of kids' cognitive abilities ↔\nit would be a grave mistake to dismiss the careful and systematic analyses compiled by Piaget\nand others before the new tests dominated the scene\nwrong与grave mistake同义\n【辨邻】 K K 段写的是 we find an emerging understanding of relations and perspectives\nthat Piaget probably did not dream of，态度方向偏向'新方法挖到了皮亚杰没料到\n的东西'，而非'不应否定皮亚杰'。\nwhen we scratch beneath the surface, we find an emerging understanding of relations and\nperspectives that Piaget probably did not dream of", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn about followers of the positive stress movement?", "options": ["A) They are usually quite sensitive to different types of stress.", "B) They hold a different view on stress from the popular one.", "C) They derive much pleasure from living a very hectic life.", "D) They gain a competitive edge by enjoying good health."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about followers of the positive stress movement 问的是关于该群体\n的客观信息，没有 infer/imply/mainly about 等推断或主旨信号。\n【拆句】题干是一般疑问式陈述句：主语 we、谓语 learn、介词短语 about followers of the\npositive stress movement 指明所问对象。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】People often discuss the dangers of too much stress, but lately a very different view of stress\nis gaining popularity: this view of stress, held by members of the positive stress movement,\nargues that stress might actually be beneficial.\n人们常讨论压力过大的危害，但最近一种截然不同的压力观正在走红：这种观点由 positive\nstress movement 的成员所持，主张压力其实可能是有益的。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nsensitive to different types of stress『对不同压力敏感』全文未提；原文只说他们对\n压力的看法不同，不是对压力本身敏感。\nB) ✓ 正确\na very different view of stress ↔ a different view on stress from the popular one\nmembers of the positive stress movement ↔ They\nB 选项就是 a very different view of stress 的标准转述，与预判完全吻合。\nC) 偷换概念\nderive much pleasure from living a very hectic life『从极忙生活中获取快乐』偷换了\n原文 looking for an edge in a competitive world 的概念——是『谋求优势』，不是\n『以忙为乐』。\nD) 与原文相反\ngain a competitive edge by enjoying good health『靠好身体获得竞争优势』因果倒\n置：原文是为求竞争优势而做极端做法，不是先有好身体再获优势。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do followers of the positive stress movement usually do to put their ideas into practice?", "options": ["A) They keep changing their living habits.", "B) They network with influential figures.", "C) They seek jobs in tech industries.", "D) They apply extreme tactics."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do followers of the positive stress movement usually do to put their ideas into\npractice 明确问『通常做什么』，是对动作的客观提取。\n【拆句】主句 What do followers...usually do，加目的状语 to put their ideas into practice。注\n意 usually 是程度词，提醒考生找的是『常态做法』而非个例。\n【定位】Inspired by influential figures in different fields, including entertainers, athletes,\nentrepreneurs and scientists, positive stress practitioners seek out some combination of\nextreme temperatures, restrictive diets, punishing exercise routines and general discomfort.\n在不同领域影响力人物（包括艺人、运动员、企业家和科学家）的启发下，positive stress\n实践者主动追求极端温度、限制性饮食、严苛锻炼和总体上的不适感的某种组合。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nkeep changing their living habits『不断改变生活习惯』全文未出现，原文讲的是采取\n固定的极端做法，不是频繁更换。\nB) 张冠李戴\nnetwork with influential figures『与影响力人物建立人脉』把原文 Inspired by\ninfluential figures『受其启发』偷换为『与其建立关系』，主体关系完全错位。\nC) 答非所问\nseek jobs in tech industries『在科技行业找工作』错读了原文 consisting largely of\ntech industry workers——后者是描述该群体『大多由科技业从业者组成』的成员构\n成，不是他们的『做法』。\nD) ✓ 正确\nextreme temperatures, restrictive diets, punishing exercise routines and general discomfort ↔\nextreme tactics\npositive stress practitioners seek out ↔ They apply\nD 用 extreme tactics 一词把原文四个并列做法整体收口，正好对应预判中的『极端\n手段』。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does Zachary Rapp say about his unconventional practices?", "options": ["A) They help him combat stress from work.", "B) They enable him to cut down living expenses.", "C) They enable him to recover from injuries and illnesses.", "D) They help him get three companies enlisted all at once."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does Zachary Rapp say about his unconventional practices 问 Rapp 自己怎么\n说，是观点转述类细节题。\n【拆句】What does Zachary Rapp say about his unconventional practices：主语 Rapp、谓语\nsay、about 后是讨论对象。无陷阱。\n【定位】He believes that these practices, which put stress on his body, actually make him feel less\nstress from work.\n他相信这些给身体施压的做法，反而让他感受到的工作压力变少。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nmake him feel less stress from work ↔ combat stress from work\nthese practices ↔ They\nA 把『让他感觉压力变小』这一结果改写为『帮助他对抗压力』这一动作，是六级细节\n题典型的『因果同义改写』。\nB) 无中生有\ncut down living expenses『降低生活开销』全文从未涉及任何与花费相关的内容。\nC) 偷换概念\nrecover from injuries and illnesses『从伤病中恢复』把原文 taking ice baths to\nrecover from sports『大学时洗冰浴以从运动中恢复』偷换为『从伤病中恢复』，且这\n是『大学时的起源』而非『现在做法的效果』。\nD) 偷换概念\nget three companies enlisted all at once『让三家公司同时上市』把原文 trying to\nget his three companies off the ground『让三家公司起步』偷换成『让三家公司上\n市』；get off the ground 与 enlist 不是一回事。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What can be inferred from the passage about day-to-day stress?", "options": ["A) It is harmful to one's physical and mental health.", "B) It does not differ in essence from positive stress.", "C) It is something everybody has to live with.", "D) It does not help build up one's tolerance."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （推断题）\n【判型】What can be inferred from the passage about day-to-day stress 中 inferred 明确点出\n题型。\n【拆句】What can be inferred from the passage about day-to-day stress：主句 What can be\ninferred、状语 from the passage、about 后是话题对象。注意 can be inferred 是被动语\n态加情态动词，等于 we can infer。\n【定位】For him, the difference between day-to-day stress, like the kind we feel when moving\napartments, and positive stress is that the latter involves pushing the body to extremes and\nforcing it to build up a tolerance.\n对他来说，日常压力（比如搬家时感受到的那种）与 positive stress 的区别在于：后者会把\n身体推向极端，迫使身体建立耐受度。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nharmful to one's physical and mental health『对身心健康有害』全文未对 day-to-\nday stress 作如此强烈的负面判断；首段提到的 dangers of too much stress 也是说\n『过大压力』而非 day-to-day。\nB) 与原文相反\ndoes not differ in essence from positive stress『与 positive stress 本质上无区别』\n恰恰与定位句 the difference between day-to-day stress...and positive stress 强调的\n『二者有区别』完全相反。\nC) 无中生有\nsomething everybody has to live with『人人必须忍受』全文未对 day-to-day stress\n是否普遍作出判断。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe latter involves pushing the body to extremes and forcing it to build up a tolerance ↔\ndoes not help build up one's tolerance\nthe difference between day-to-day stress...and positive stress is that the latter ↔ It\nthe latter 独占 build up a tolerance，按对比逻辑前者就缺失该特征——D 正好把这一\n反向推论说出来。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What do some medical professionals think of positive stress?", "options": ["A) Its true effect remains to be verified.", "B) Its side effect should not be ignored.", "C) Its effect varies considerably from person to person.", "D) Its practitioners should not take it as a form of therapy."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do some medical professionals think of positive stress 问的是该群体的客观观\n点，可在文中找到对应转述句，不是全文态度题。\n【拆句】What do some medical professionals think of positive stress：注意 some 是范围词\n——只是『一些』医生，不代表全体，提示选项里不要选全称表述。\n【定位】But it is important to note that not everyone agrees with these practitioners; indeed, some\nmedical professionals argue that positive stress is not for everyone, and that it might even be\ndangerous for people who are unhealthy or older.\n但需要注意的是，并非所有人都赞同这些实践者；事实上，一些医学专业人士主张 positive\nstress 并不适合所有人，对体质不佳或年长者甚至可能有危险。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nIts true effect remains to be verified『真正效果有待验证』全文未提及医生对\npositive stress 效果是否已被验证的判断。\nB) 偷换概念\nIts side effect should not be ignored『不应忽视其副作用』把 might even be\ndangerous 偷换为 side effect（副作用）；危险不等于副作用，副作用是医学专门\n术语。\nC) ✓ 正确\nnot for everyone ↔ varies considerably from person to person\nit might even be dangerous for people who are unhealthy or older ↔ varies considerably\n原文一边否定『对所有人都适用』，一边指出特定群体可能有危险——这两点合起来就\n是『效果因人而异』，C 精准命中。\nD) 张冠李戴\nIts practitioners should not take it as a form of therapy『实践者不应把它当作疗\n法』把 some even call it a form of therapy『有人甚至称其为一种疗法』错配到医生\n头上，且未在医生口中出现这种反对意见。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author say sounds ironic?", "options": ["A) Some predators may often turn out to be prey of other predators.", "B) Hunting may also be a solution to the problem caused by hunting.", "C) The species of prey animals continue to vary despite humans' hunting.", "D) The number of prey animals keeps rising despite environmental change."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say sounds ironic? 用 what 直接索取作者点名为讽刺的那件\n事，无 infer/imply/main idea 字样。\n【拆句】What does the author say sounds ironic? 是宾语前置的间接引语：what 是 sounds\nironic 的逻辑主语，the author say 作插入语；还原即 the author says (that) what\nsounds ironic。无陷阱，但要留意它问的是\"被作者认为讽刺的事\"，不是\"讽刺地……\"。\n【定位】And, ironic as it sounds, since humans have wiped out many animal predators, some see\nhunting as a natural way to reduce the herds of prey animals that now reproduce beyond the\nenvironment's carrying capacity.\n而且，听起来虽然讽刺：既然人类已经消灭了许多动物天敌，有人就把狩猎看作一种天然手\n段，用来削减那些如今繁殖数量已超过环境承载力的猎物种群。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nSome predators may often turn out to be prey of other predators. 谈\"天敌也是别\n的天敌的猎物\"的食物链问题，原文只提 humans 既是 predator 又是 prey，并未讨论\n天敌之间的相互捕食。\nB) ✓ 正确\nsince humans have wiped out many animal predators, some see hunting as a natural way to\nreduce the herds of prey animals that now reproduce beyond the environment's carrying capacity\n↔ Hunting may also be a solution to the problem caused by hunting.\n预判\"制造问题的手段反过来变成解决问题的手段\"与 Hunting may also be a solution\nto the problem caused by hunting. 完全等值——problem 对应 overpopulation,\nsolution 对应 a natural way to reduce, caused by hunting 对应 humans have\nwiped out predators。\nC) 答非所问\nThe species of prey animals continue to vary despite humans' hunting. 说\"猎物物\n种持续多样化\"，原文未谈物种多样化方向；且即使为真，也回答的是\"猎物物种变化\"而\n非\"作者觉得讽刺的事\"。\nD) 偷换概念\nThe number of prey animals keeps rising despite environmental change. 把原文\n的\"超过环境承载力 (beyond the environment's carrying capacity)\"偷换成\"despite\nenvironmental change（不顾环境变化）\"，并丢弃了\"因人类消灭天敌\"这层因果。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does Glenn Kirk think of charging hunters license fees?", "options": ["A) It keeps game population under control.", "B) It turns hunting into a sport of the rich.", "C) It leads to ecological imbalance.", "D) It helps stop killing for pleasure."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does Glenn Kirk think of charging hunters license fees? 索取一个具名人物\n对一个具体做法的明确观点，原文必有对应表述。\n【拆句】What does Glenn Kirk think of charging hunters license fees? 中 charging hunters\nlicense fees 是动名词短语作 of 的宾语，charge sb. sth. 即\"向某人收取某费\"。基础薄弱\n考生易把 charging 误读为现在分词\"正在收费的\"，要看清它是名词性结构。\n【定位】He adds that, despite hunters' claims that hunting keeps wildlife populations in balance,\nhunters' license fees are used to \"manipulate a few game species into overpopulation at the\nexpense of a much larger number of non-game species, resulting in the loss of biological\ndiversity, genetic integrity and ecological balance.\"\n他补充说：尽管猎人声称狩猎能让野生动物种群保持平衡，但猎人的执照费实际上被用\n来\"操纵少数狩猎物种过度繁殖，代价是数量大得多的非狩猎物种，结果造成生物多样性、\n遗传完整性和生态平衡的丧失\"。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt keeps game population under control. 说\"让狩猎物种数量受控\"，但 Kirk 恰恰指\n出 license fees 是 manipulate a few game species into overpopulation——是制造\n过度繁殖，而非控制数量。\nB) 无中生有\nIt turns hunting into a sport of the rich. 说\"把狩猎变成富人的运动\"，原文从未提及\n收费让狩猎变贵或仅富人可及。\nC) ✓ 正确\nresulting in the loss of biological diversity, genetic integrity and ecological balance ↔\nIt leads to ecological imbalance.\n预判\"执照费导致生态失衡\"完整对应 It leads to ecological imbalance.，且把原文三项\n后果（diversity / integrity / balance）中最具概括性的 ecological balance 留作落\n点，正是六级正确项的典型做法。\nD) 与原文相反\nIt helps stop killing for pleasure. 说\"有助于停止为娱乐而杀\"，与 Kirk 全篇的批评立\n场矛盾——他在 P2 上半段还指责 hunters kill for pleasure，根本不可能认为执照费能\n解决这件事。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What is the argument of the Humane Society of the United States against hunting?", "options": ["A) Overpopulation is not an issue for most hunted animals.", "B) Hunting deprives animal populations of their food sources.", "C) Many birds and small animals are being irrationally killed.", "D) Hunting is universally acknowledged as a savage behavior."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is the argument of the Humane Society of the United States against\nhunting? 直接索取一个组织的反方理据。\n【拆句】What is the argument of the Humane Society of the United States against hunting?\n是 What is the N? 的判断句；against hunting 修饰 argument，意为\"反对狩猎的论点\"。\n结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】According to the Humane Society of the United States, the vast majority of hunted species—\nsuch as waterfowl, rabbits, upland birds and mourning doves—\"provide minimal nutrition\nand do not require population control.\"\n美国人道协会指出：绝大多数被猎杀的物种——例如水禽、兔子、高地鸟和哀鸽——\"营养\n价值极低，且并不需要被控制数量\"。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe vast majority of hunted species…\"…do not require population control.\" ↔\nOverpopulation is not an issue for most hunted animals.\n预判\"大多数被猎物种不需控数\"与 Overpopulation is not an issue for most hunted\nanimals. 完全对应：not require control（不需要控）= overpopulation is not an\nissue（不存在过剩问题）。\nB) 无中生有\nHunting deprives animal populations of their food sources. 说\"狩猎剥夺了动物种\n群的食物来源\"，原文 P3 全段未谈\"食物来源\"问题。\nC) 张冠李戴\nMany birds and small animals are being irrationally killed. 这种\"残忍/非理性\"表述\n属于 P2 中 Glenn Kirk 的立场（irrationally cruel），而非 P3 中 Humane Society 的\n论点。\nD) 过度推断\nHunting is universally acknowledged as a savage behavior. 把\"反方观点\"夸张为\"普\n遍公认\"，universally 远超 P3 的具体表述，事实上 P2 已说明只是 some 反对者持此\n立场。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "When is hunting morally justifiable according to Gary E. Varner?", "options": ["A) When it benefits animals and their ecosystem.", "B) When it serves both human and animal interests.", "C) When it is indispensable to humans' subsistence.", "D) When it stabilizes the population of animal species."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 When is hunting morally justifiable according to Gary E. Varner? 用 when 索取一\n个明确条件，原文必有 Varner 给出的判定标准。\n【拆句】When is hunting morally justifiable according to Gary E. Varner? 是 when 引导的特殊\n疑问句，morally justifiable 是\"副词+形容词\"作表语，意为\"在道德上可被辩护的\"。结构清\n晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】Hunting \"designed to secure the aggregate welfare of the target species, the integrity of its\necosystem, or both\"—what Varner terms \"therapeutic hunting\"—is defensible, while\nsubsistence and sport hunting—both of which only benefit human beings—are not.\nVarner 称那种\"旨在保障目标物种整体福祉、其生态系统完整性、或二者兼顾\"的狩猎为\"治\n疗性狩猎\"，认为它是可被辩护的；而生计性狩猎和运动性狩猎——这两者只让人类得益\n——则不可辩护。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ndesigned to secure the aggregate welfare of the target species, the integrity of its ecosystem, or\nboth\n↔ When it benefits animals and their ecosystem.\n预判\"为目标物种与生态系统好\"与 When it benefits animals and their ecosystem. 一\n一对应——target species→animals, its ecosystem→their ecosystem, secure\nwelfare/integrity→benefits。\nB) 与原文相反\nWhen it serves both human and animal interests. 把答案纳入\"既为人也为动物\"，但\n原文 Varner 明确说仅 only benefit human beings 的 subsistence/sport hunting 是\n不可辩护的；可辩护的那类不包含\"为人类利益\"。\nC) 与原文相反\nWhen it is indispensable to humans' subsistence. 直接为 subsistence hunting 辩\n护，而 Varner 明确把 subsistence hunting 列为\"不可辩护\"那一类。\nD) 以偏概全\nWhen it stabilizes the population of animal species. 只截取\"控数\"这一面（接近 P1\n论调），而 Varner 的判据更宽——welfare、ecosystem integrity 都算；且\"stabilize\npopulation\"在 P4 并无对应表述。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-07-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What concept are green leaders trying to promote?", "options": ["A) Effective protection of animal habitats.", "B) Strict control over urban development.", "C) Coordinated efforts of hunters and environmentalists.", "D) A compromise between development and animal protection."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What concept are green leaders trying to promote? 索取一个具体主张，原文末段\n必有对应表述。\n【拆句】What concept are green leaders trying to promote? 是 what 作宾语前置的现在进行时\n疑问句，are trying to promote 表\"正在努力推动\"。注意 concept 是抽象名词，原文给的\n将是一个\"主张/理念\"而非具体行动。\n【定位】Perhaps matching the trend among the public, green leaders are increasingly advocating\ncooperation between hunters and environmental groups: After all, both deplore urban sprawl\nand habitat destruction.\n或许与公众的趋势相呼应，环保领袖们越来越主张猎人与环保团体之间展开合作：毕竟，双\n方都谴责城市无序扩张和栖息地破坏。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nEffective protection of animal habitats. 只截取末句 habitat destruction 的一个侧\n面，而 green leaders 推动的核心是\"双方合作\"——栖息地保护只是合作理由，不是他\n们倡导的概念。\nB) 以偏概全\nStrict control over urban development. 同样只截取 urban sprawl 一个侧面，且\ngreen leaders 并未单独倡导\"严控城市发展\"，而是把\"deplore urban sprawl\"作为双方\n合作的共同立场。\nC) ✓ 正确\ngreen leaders are increasingly advocating cooperation between hunters and environmental\ngroups\n↔ Coordinated efforts of hunters and environmentalists.\n预判\"猎人与环保派联手合作\"与 Coordinated efforts of hunters and\nenvironmentalists. 一一对应，且选项的抽象度（concept）正好匹配题干 What\nconcept 的提问层级。\nD) 无中生有\nA compromise between development and animal protection. 引入\"发展 vs 动物保\n护的妥协\"二元对立，原文从未涉及\"开发派 vs 保护派的妥协\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
